#: help_topics.py:22
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:22
msgid ""
"\n"
"Bookbench edits EPUB and KEPUB files for KOReader: **chapter names** and "
"levels in the table of contents, **blurbs**, **covers** and **metadata** "
"(title, authors, series …). It also manages your own **dictionaries**, "
"backs up the KOReader settings of the **e-reader** and, on Linux and macOS, "
"shows a book in a **preview** with the settings from the e-reader.\n"
"\n"
"**Workflow**\n"
"- Open a folder (Ctrl+O): the books folder on the e-reader, a copy on the "
"computer or the Calibre library.\n"
"- In **Table of contents** check the suggestions, change them if needed, "
"apply them.\n"
"- In **Metadata** (with blurb and cover) fill in what is missing, apply "
"it.\n"
"- **Save** (Ctrl+S) writes all applied changes to the files. A backup of "
"every file is made first.\n"
"\n"
"Only the table of contents and the metadata (and the cover image) are "
"changed, never the book text. Reading positions and highlights in KOReader "
"stay valid.\n"
"\n"
"**More**\n"
"- [[status]] - what the coloured dots and the small pictograms mean\n"
"- [[tasten]]\n"
"- [[dateien]] - where settings, caches and backups are\n"
"- [[kosten]] - what is free, what costs money, what leaves the computer\n"
"- [[bereiche]] - one chapter per area\n"
"- [[einstellungen]] - every setting explained\n"
"- [[fehler]]\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:42
msgid "Status and markers"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:42
msgid ""
"\n"
"The strip to the right of the book lists shows the status of each book:\n"
"- **red** ●: changed, not yet applied\n"
"- **yellow** ●: applied, not yet saved\n"
"- **green** ●: saved (since opening)\n"
"\n"
"Next to the dot small **pictograms** show what is missing or needs a look - "
"each list only those that belong to its area. The **sidebar** shows the "
"same pictograms per area, each with the number of books.\n"
"\n"
"- **Torn page** (red): the file cannot be read.\n"
"- **Question mark** (orange): questionable chapter names, not yet decided.\n"
"- **Light bulb** (blue): chapter suggestions waiting to be applied.\n"
"- **Speech bubble** (grey): no blurb.\n"
"- **Picture** (grey): no cover. **Small picture with corners** (orange): "
"cover lower than the limit ([[einst_cover]]).\n"
"- **Not-equal sign** (orange): title, authors, series or number differ from "
"Calibre.\n"
"- **Label with !** (orange): missing language or an invalid ISBN.\n"
"- **Two books with a dashed gap** (orange, sidebar and Book series only): "
"series with missing numbers ([[reihen]]).\n"
"- **Floppy disk** (yellow, sidebar only): books with changes that are not "
"saved yet.\n"
"- In Dictionary and E-reader: **book** (words looked up without result), "
"**e-reader with !** (dictionaries missing or outdated), **tick** "
"(connected).\n"
"- In Library and E-reader: **two arrows** (blue, books to sync with the "
"e-reader) and **arrows against each other** (orange, changed on the "
"computer and on the e-reader), see [[kobo_abgleich]].\n"
"\n"
"Colours: red = error, orange = check it, grey = missing, blue = "
"information, yellow = not saved. Hovering over the strip or a sidebar entry "
"explains the markers. If the markers of an area do not fit into one line of "
"the sidebar, they move to a second line below the name.\n"
"\n"
"In the **library** dot and pictograms are in a column of their own, "
"**Status**, always on the left: a right-click on a column header hides or "
"shows it, its width can be dragged, a click on it sorts (unreadable files "
"first, then the books with the most markers). The title bar shows an "
"asterisk (*) after the version as long as something is not saved.\n"
"\n"
"**Backgrounds**\n"
"- In the chapter table questionable rows are **red**; a click shows the "
"reason in the status bar. **Yellowish**: only a number, no name found.\n"
"\n"
"The strip can be switched off: Settings > General > Interface "
"([[einst_oberflaeche]]). Single pictograms can be hidden per place "
"(sidebar, book lists, status column of the library): [[einst_marken]].\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:71
msgid "Keyboard shortcuts"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:71
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Everywhere**\n"
"- **Ctrl+O** open a folder, **F5** reload it\n"
"- **Ctrl+I** add books ([[bibliothek_hinzufuegen]])\n"
"- **Ctrl+S** save\n"
"- **Ctrl+Enter** apply (current book, or the dictionary form)\n"
"- **Ctrl+F** search field of the current area (Table of contents, Metadata, "
"Book series, Dictionary), otherwise of the library; **Esc** in a search "
"field empties it\n"
"- **Ctrl+B** collapse and expand the sidebar\n"
"- **Ctrl+1 … 8** choose an area in the order of the sidebar (8: Preview, "
"Linux/macOS only)\n"
"- **F1** help for the current area; in the settings: help for the page "
"shown\n"
"\n"
"**Chapter table**\n"
"- **Double-click** or **F2** edits the new name, **Enter** confirms, "
"**Esc** cancels, **Del** restores the original\n"
"- **Tab** / **Shift+Tab** (or **Alt+→** / **Alt+←**): level down / up\n"
"\n"
"**Dialogs**\n"
"- **Enter** = OK, **Esc** = cancel; in the cover choice a **double-click** "
"takes a cover at once\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:89
msgid "Files and folders"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:89
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Settings folder** (Windows: %APPDATA%\\Bookbench, Linux: "
"~/.config/bookbench)\n"
"- **einstellungen.json**: all settings, including the API key (plain "
"text).\n"
"- **tocfix_entscheidungen.json**: applied chapter names, so they are "
"suggested again for other copies of the same book.\n"
"- **blurbs_cache.json**: looked-up blurbs and what was already tried "
"without success.\n"
"- **vorschau/**: the profile of the preview (Linux/macOS).\n"
"- **einlese-cache/**: what was read from the books, per library, so "
"unchanged books open faster ([[einst_bibliothek]]).\n"
"Until version 0.23 the two JSON files were next to the program; until 0.24 "
"the folder was called Kobo-Werkstatt (the old name). Both are copied over "
"once. Settings > Advanced shows the paths and opens the folder "
"([[einst_erweitert]]).\n"
"\n"
"**Backup folder** (default: Bookbench Backups in your user folder)\n"
"- Backups of the books before each save: <name>_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS.epub.\n"
"- **KOReader/**: backups of the KOReader settings from the E-reader area.\n"
"- **Wörterbücher/**: dictionary files before they are built again.\n"
"\n"
"**In the book folder**\n"
"- Nothing is created next to the books; the EPUB files themselves change "
"when saving. A library on this computer ([[bibliothek_rechner]]) gets a "
"hidden folder .bookbench with library.json (folder scheme, e-reader).\n"
"\n"
"**On the e-reader**\n"
"- Only what you copy or restore yourself: KOReader settings (in the "
"KOReader folder, e.g. .adds/koreader on a Kobo, [[kobo_geraete]]), "
"dictionaries (data/dict in the KOReader folder), and the books when the "
"e-reader folder is opened and saved.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:109
msgid "Costs and privacy"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:109
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Free, without a key**\n"
"- Google Books and Open Library: blurbs ([[klappentexte_nachschlagen]]) and "
"covers ([[cover_aendern]]). Sent: ISBN, title and author of the book. "
"Google Books without a key shares one daily quota with everyone else; an "
"own key (also free) gives you your own ([[einst_online]]).\n"
"- Everything else in the program runs on the computer only, apart from the "
"update check (GitHub, can be switched off: [[einst_updates]]), building a "
"dictionary from a wiki (the wiki you enter) and, when you agree, installing "
"a missing package (pypi.org). No telemetry.\n"
"\n"
"**Costs money: Claude (Anthropic API)**\n"
"- Blurbs that the free sources do not have: about 1 to 2 cents per book, "
"with web search about 1 cent more.\n"
"- Dictionary: summarizing wiki articles, cross-checking, filling gaps with "
"Claude: from a few cents per entry; before larger jobs the estimated cost "
"is asked.\n"
"- You need your own API key (console.anthropic.com), billed by Anthropic. "
"Without a key nothing goes to Claude.\n"
"- Sent: title, author, series and language of the book, the profile's "
"instructions; for the dictionary the term, topic and article text. **Book "
"text never leaves the computer** - searching the books for names happens "
"locally.\n"
"\n"
"**Avoiding costs**\n"
"- Results are kept in the cache; looking up again costs nothing "
"([[dateien]]).\n"
"- From Calibre costs nothing and is used first.\n"
"- Look up all only asks Claude for books the free sources do not know, and "
"asks before it starts.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:125
msgid "Areas"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:125
msgid ""
"\n"
"The sidebar on the left switches between the areas (Ctrl+1 … 8):\n"
"- [[bibliothek]] - all books in a table\n"
"- [[kapitel]] - table of contents: chapter names and levels\n"
"- [[metadaten]] - title, authors, series, publisher, language, ISBN, tags, "
"own fields, blurb and cover\n"
"- [[reihen]] - all series with their books, missing numbers and reading "
"state\n"
"- [[sammlungen]] - KOReader's collections: own lists of books in your own "
"order\n"
"- [[woerterbuch]] - your own dictionaries\n"
"- [[kobo]] - the connected e-reader: backups, dictionaries, ejecting\n"
"- [[vorschau]] - a book in the desktop KOReader (Linux/macOS)\n"
"\n"
"The toolbar has the buttons of the current area on the left; on the right, "
"separated, Open, Reload and Save (only in Library, Table of contents, "
"Metadata and Book series - the areas with the books of the folder) and "
"Help. [[speichern]] applies to all book areas together. The **Edit** menu "
"only holds the commands of the area shown; Dictionary, E-reader and Preview "
"have menus of their own.\n"
"\n"
"The **book lists** on the left of Table of contents and Metadata (and the "
"list of the dictionary; in the Preview the left column) can be made wider "
"or narrower by dragging the divider to their right; the width is "
"remembered, a double-click on the divider restores the default. A name that "
"does not fit is shown completely in a tooltip when the mouse is over it, "
"together with what its pictograms mean.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:140
msgid "Search and filters in the lists"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:140
msgid ""
"\n"
"Above the book lists of **Table of contents** and **Metadata** there is a "
"search field and a filter (the Preview has a book selection instead of a "
"list since 1.4). Each list has its own; they do not affect each other or "
"the library.\n"
"\n"
"- **Search** finds parts of the title, series or author, case does not "
"matter. **×** in the field empties the search, **Esc** search and filter.\n"
"- When a filter is active, the line above says how many books are shown: "
"\"Books: 3 of 12\".\n"
"- The book you are working on stays selected as long as it fits. If it no "
"longer fits, the next fitting book below is selected (otherwise the one "
"above).\n"
"- **Apply** alone does not refill the list, so the book does not vanish "
"while you work on it. Only changing search or filter, **Reload** or **Apply "
"all** takes it out.\n"
"- If a hidden book is opened from elsewhere (double-click in the library), "
"search and filter of that list are reset.\n"
"- Ctrl+F jumps to the search field of the current area.\n"
"\n"
"**Filters**\n"
"- **Table of contents**: With suggestions, Questionable names (⚠, not yet "
"decided), Some without names (single entries only with a number), Changed "
"chapters (applied or edited, not yet saved).\n"
"- **Metadata**: Changed, No blurb, Blurb only in Calibre, Differs from "
"Calibre, No author sort, No language, No ISBN, Edited in KOReader, No "
"cover, Small cover (below the limit in [[einst_cover]]).\n"
"\n"
"Search and filters are not remembered; after a restart all books are shown "
"again.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:156
msgid ""
"\n"
"All books of the opened folder (with all subfolders) with series, number, "
"author, state of the chapters, blurb and cover.\n"
"\n"
"- A click on a column header sorts (▲ ascending, ▼ descending), a second "
"click reverses. Moving, hiding and widths of the columns: "
"[[bibliothek_spalten]].\n"
"- **Search** filters by title, series and author; the **filter** shows only "
"changed books, unreadable files or books whose series is not in the EPUB. "
"Filters by chapters, blurbs and covers are above the lists of those areas "
"([[suche_filter]]).\n"
"- **Double-click** opens the book in the Table of contents area; under "
"Settings > General > Interface it can be Metadata, the Preview or KOReader "
"instead ([[einst_oberflaeche]]).\n"
"- **Right-click** on a book: open it in Table of contents, Metadata, the "
"Preview or start it in KOReader (Preview and KOReader not on Windows). The "
"entry marked \"Double-click\" is the one the double-click uses. **Show "
"series** jumps to the book in [[reihen]]; **Move to series …** and **Add to "
"collection** work on all selected books ([[reihen]], [[sammlungen]]).\n"
"- **Several books**: Ctrl+click and Shift+click select more than one; "
"right-click > **Edit metadata of n books** takes them to the Metadata area "
"for editing together ([[metadaten_mehrere]]), **Remove advert text from n "
"books** works on just those; **Look up metadata …** opens the look-up "
"window for the selected books ([[metadaten_nachschlagen]]).\n"
"- **New books**: File > Add books (Ctrl+I) or drag them onto the window "
"([[bibliothek_hinzufuegen]]); a library on this computer instead of the "
"e-reader: [[bibliothek_rechner]].\n"
"- **Advert lines** like OceanofPDF.com: Edit > Remove advert text … "
"([[bibliothek_cleaner]]).\n"
"- **Duplicates**: Edit > Find duplicates … ([[bibliothek_duplikate]]).\n"
"\n"
"**Columns**\n"
"- **Chapters**: **suggestions** (the book text has chapter names the table "
"of contents lacks), **without names** (only numbers, nothing in the text "
"either), **no names found** (only a few matches - probably coincidence, so "
"nothing is suggested), **ok**, **ok (2 without names)** (almost all have "
"names, single ones only a number, e.g. a prologue).\n"
"- **Blurb**: where it comes from (Google Books, Calibre, a website), "
"\"yes\" if unknown, \"in Calibre\" if only Calibre has one, \"changed\".\n"
"- **Cover**: size of the title image in pixels, \"changed\" for a new one "
"not yet saved.\n"
"- **Series**: from the EPUB; if it is only in Calibre or KOReader's data, "
"the status bar says where from ([[bibliothek_calibre]]).\n"
"- **Publisher**, **Language**, **ISBN**, **Published**: hidden at first; "
"show them with a right-click on a header or View > Library columns.\n"
"- **Read status**, **Last read**, **Reading time**: what KOReader knows "
"about your reading - unread, 53 %, finished, on hold "
"([[bibliothek_lesestand]]). Last read and Reading time are hidden at "
"first.\n"
"- **Status** (left): the coloured dot and the pictograms of each book "
"([[status]]).\n"
"\n"
"**Filters** All books, Changed (anything not yet saved, or saved since "
"opening), Unreadable files, No series in the EPUB; Unread, Currently "
"reading, Finished, On hold from KOReader's reading state "
"([[bibliothek_lesestand]]).\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:179
msgid "Adding books"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:179
msgid ""
"\n"
"File > **Add books …** (Ctrl+I) opens a window for new books. Put EPUB or "
"KEPUB files in with **Add files …**, **Add folder …** (with all subfolders) "
"or simply drag them onto the window or the main window.\n"
"\n"
"**The list**\n"
"- One row per file with title, author, series, number and where it will go "
"(**→** path in the library, following the folder scheme, "
"[[bibliothek_ordnerschema]]).\n"
"- **Action** per book: Add, Skip, Replace existing, Keep both. Several rows "
"at once: select them and use **Set action for selected …**.\n"
"- **⧉ Possible duplicate**: the library already has the book, recognised in "
"this order by the Calibre ID, the ISBN, or title and first author (ignoring "
"case, punctuation and a leading \"The\"). Duplicates are set to Skip; you "
"decide.\n"
"- **Replace existing** overwrites the existing file **at its path**, so "
"KOReader keeps the reading position and highlights (they are stored next to "
"the file name). A backup is made first ([[speichern]]).\n"
"- **Keep both** adds the new copy next to it; if the path is taken, \" "
"(2)\" is added to the name.\n"
"- Unreadable files are shown with ⚠ and skipped.\n"
"\n"
"**Checking metadata**\n"
"On the right: title, authors, author sort, series, number and ISBN of the "
"selected book. Changes go into the copy in the library, the original file "
"stays as it is; the target path follows at once. Everything else (blurb, "
"cover, own fields) afterwards in the Metadata area.\n"
"\n"
"**Add n books** copies the books, then the library is read again (only the "
"new books really, thanks to the reading cache, [[einst_bibliothek]]) and "
"the first new book is selected. Unsaved changes in the library must be "
"saved or discarded first.\n"
"\n"
"Bookbench does not convert to KEPUB: EPUB is fine for KOReader, existing "
"KEPUB files are taken as they are.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:197 settings.py:369 settings.py:994 settings.py:1001
msgid "Folder scheme"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:197
msgid ""
"\n"
"The folder scheme says where a new book goes inside the library, e.g. "
"**{author_sort}/[{series}/][{series_index:2} - ]{title}** gives \"Abnett, "
"Dan/The Horus Heresy/01 - Horus Rising.epub\", and for a book without a "
"series \"Abnett, Dan/Ravenor.epub\".\n"
"\n"
"- Placeholders like in Calibre: {title}, {authors}, {author} (first "
"author), {author_sort}, {series}, {series_index}, {publisher}, {language}, "
"{isbn}.\n"
"- **{series_index:2}** fills up to two digits with zeros (3 → 03; 1.5 stays "
"1.5).\n"
"- **[…]**: the part in square brackets is left out when a placeholder in it "
"is empty.\n"
"- Characters that Windows or the e-reader do not allow (: ? * \" < > | \\ "
"/) become _.\n"
"\n"
"**Where the scheme comes from**\n"
"1. Saved for this library (Settings > Books > Library and import, "
"[[einst_bibliothek]]).\n"
"2. Otherwise detected from the books: Bookbench puts the metadata of every "
"book into its path and looks for the scheme most books follow. It is used "
"if at least half of the books match, and saved with the first books added.\n"
"3. Otherwise the default from the settings.\n"
"\n"
"Existing books are never moved or renamed - not even when the scheme "
"changes.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:212
msgid "Library on this computer"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:212
msgid ""
"\n"
"Since 2.1 the main copy of the books can live in a folder on this computer, "
"like in Calibre, and the e-reader gets them from there. Editing directly on "
"the e-reader keeps working as before.\n"
"\n"
"**New library from e-reader** (File menu): copies all books of the "
"connected e-reader into a folder on this computer, with the same folder "
"structure. KOReader's .sdr folders (reading positions, highlights) stay on "
"the e-reader. The folder scheme is detected from the books "
"([[bibliothek_ordnerschema]]) and the new library is opened. Running it "
"again into the same folder only copies what is missing or different.\n"
"\n"
"**.bookbench folder**: the library folder gets a hidden folder .bookbench "
"with library.json (folder scheme, which e-reader belongs to it). It lies in "
"the library folder on purpose: if the folder moves or is synced between "
"Windows and Linux, this comes along.\n"
"\n"
"New or changed books get to the e-reader (and back) with the sync in the "
"E-reader area ([[kobo_abgleich]]).\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:221
msgid "Removing advert text"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:221
msgid ""
"\n"
"Some books carry lines like \"OceanofPDF.com\" at the end of each chapter. "
"**Edit > Remove advert text …** (in the Library area) searches all books "
"for them, **right-click a book in the library > Remove advert text …** only "
"that book.\n"
"\n"
"**The window**\n"
"- One entry per book with its places, each with the text around it: …before "
"⟦found⟧ after….\n"
"- **Removes**: *whole <div>* (or p, a …) - the text stands in a container "
"of its own, the container goes too, so no empty line stays behind. *Text "
"only* - the text stands in a paragraph with other content, only the text "
"goes.\n"
"- Everything is checked; untick places or whole books you want to keep.\n"
"- **Remove n places** writes the books right away (a backup is made first, "
"[[speichern]]) and reads them again. Books with unsaved changes are "
"skipped; save or discard first, then **Search again**.\n"
"- **Patterns …** opens the settings ([[einst_cleaner]]).\n"
"\n"
"A **link** whose address matches counts too, however it is labelled: it is "
"shown as label → address (e.g. \"Download more books → "
"https://oceanofpdf.com\") and removed as a whole. Matches in other "
"attributes (an image file name, a class) do not count.\n"
"\n"
"Text in the head of a file (e.g. the title of a chapter file) is not "
"touched. KOReader may shift a bookmark or the reading position in the same "
"chapter by a line.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:235
msgid "Finding duplicates"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:235
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Edit > Find duplicates …** (in the library) looks for the same book more "
"than once in the opened folder. Two books count as the same if they have "
"the same **Calibre ID**, the same **ISBN**, the same **title and first "
"author** (articles, punctuation and upper/lower case do not matter) or the "
"same **file content** (a copy under another name). Books linked that way "
"form one group - an EPUB and a KEPUB of the same book, and a copy of one of "
"them, end up together.\n"
"\n"
"**Also compare file contents** reads every file that has exactly the size "
"of another one; on the e-reader over USB that takes a moment. Without it, "
"copies are only found by ID, ISBN or title.\n"
"\n"
"Each group shows its copies with folder, format, size, date, read status, "
"state of the chapters and cover, so you can choose which to keep. **Nothing "
"is checked.** Check the copies to delete, then **Delete n copies …**: it "
"asks first (and warns if all copies of a book are checked), backs up each "
"file ([[speichern]]), and reads the library again. A book with unsaved "
"changes cannot be checked. A double-click (or Show in Library) selects the "
"copy in the library.\n"
"\n"
"**Collections** ([[sammlungen]]) that contain a deleted copy get a kept "
"copy of the same group instead (if any is left). KOReader's data next to a "
"book (.sdr folder: reading position, highlights) is not deleted; in a "
"library on the computer the next sync offers to delete the book on the "
"e-reader too, not checked ([[kobo_abgleich]]).\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:244
msgid "Reading state from KOReader"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:244
msgid ""
"\n"
"Bookbench shows what KOReader knows about your reading. It only **reads** "
"it and never writes anything back - mark a book as finished in KOReader "
"itself.\n"
"\n"
"**Where it comes from**\n"
"- **Status, progress, rating, highlights** from the book's data folder next "
"to it (Book.sdr/metadata.epub.lua): KOReader writes \"reading\", "
"\"finished\" or \"on hold\" (older KOReader: \"abandoned\") there, and how "
"far you are. A book without such a folder was never opened: **unread**.\n"
"- **Reading time, pages read, last read** from KOReader's reading "
"statistics (settings/statistics.sqlite3 in the KOReader folder of the "
"e-reader), if the statistics plugin is on. Bookbench finds the book there "
"by KOReader's own checksum, otherwise by title and first author.\n"
"\n"
"**When it is read**\n"
"- **Folder on the e-reader** opened (or the e-reader itself): fresh on "
"every reading-in (F5).\n"
"- **Library on this computer** linked with the e-reader "
"([[kobo_abgleich]]): when the e-reader is connected, Bookbench reads the "
"state of all books there and notes it in the library's library.json. "
"Without the e-reader you see that note; the library header says from when "
"(the tooltip names the e-reader). Books that were not on the e-reader count "
"as unread.\n"
"- Other folders: only if .sdr folders lie next to the books (a copy of the "
"e-reader), without statistics.\n"
"\n"
"**Where you see it**\n"
"- Library: columns **Read status** (unread, 53 %, finished, on hold), "
"**Last read** and **Reading time** (the last two hidden at first: "
"right-click on a header), sortable; filters **Unread**, **Currently "
"reading**, **Finished**, **On hold**.\n"
"- Status bar when a book is selected, and the line above the form in the "
"Metadata area: finished on …, last read, reading time, pages read, rating "
"stars, highlights.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:260
msgid "Columns: sorting, moving, widths"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:260
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Sorting**\n"
"- A click on a column header sorts by it, a second click reverses. The "
"arrow ▲ (ascending) or ▼ (descending) shows the column.\n"
"- Series sorts by series, then number, then title; No. by number; Cover by "
"the height in pixels (no cover first); Status puts unreadable files first, "
"then the books with the most markers.\n"
"- Right-click on a header: **Sort ascending** / **Sort descending** for "
"this column.\n"
"\n"
"**Moving columns**\n"
"- Drag a header sideways (hold the mouse button or touchpad): the column "
"moves along and stays where you release it.\n"
"- Without dragging: right-click on the header > **Move \"…\" left** / "
"**right**.\n"
"\n"
"**Showing and hiding**\n"
"- Right-click on a header, or View > **Library columns**: one tick per "
"column. A shown column comes back at its usual place. The last column "
"cannot be hidden.\n"
"- **Status** is always the first column and cannot be moved; it can be "
"hidden like the others.\n"
"\n"
"**Widths**\n"
"- Drag the line between two headers. The widths stay exactly as drawn, also "
"after resizing the window or a restart; no column stretches by itself. If "
"the columns are wider than the window, a scroll bar appears below.\n"
"- As long as no width has been drawn, the columns are fitted to the window "
"whenever its size changes.\n"
"- **Status** is as wide as the book with the most pictograms needs, unless "
"you drag it.\n"
"- **Fit columns to the window** spreads the visible columns over the width, "
"keeping their proportions.\n"
"- **Reset columns**: usual order, all columns, default widths fitted to the "
"window.\n"
"\n"
"Order, shown columns, widths and sorting are remembered in the settings.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:283
msgid "Calibre and series"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:283
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Series and blurbs from Calibre** (toolbar of the library) takes series, "
"number and blurb from Calibre's **metadata.calibre**, but only where the "
"EPUB has nothing - existing data is never overwritten. Then save as usual "
"(Ctrl+S); after that the data is in the book itself, and KOReader, "
"Bookshelf and the reading software of the e-reader see it without Calibre "
"too.\n"
"\n"
"**Where the file is**\n"
"Calibre writes metadata.calibre into the root folder of the e-reader when "
"transferring books. It is searched for in the opened folder and in all "
"folders above it; for a copy on the computer simply copy the file into the "
"books folder too. Books are matched by path, file name, Calibre UUID or "
"title and author.\n"
"\n"
"**Where a series comes from**\n"
"- From the EPUB (Calibre or EPUB 3 notation).\n"
"- Only in metadata.calibre: shown, the status bar says so, the filter **No "
"series in the EPUB** lists these books.\n"
"- Only in KOReader's own book data (metadata edited in KOReader, folder "
".sdr next to the book): shown too - this only works if the folder with the "
".sdr folders is opened, e.g. the e-reader directly.\n"
"\n"
"In the Metadata area, **From Calibre** next to the blurb puts Calibre's "
"description of a single book into the text field.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:296
msgid ""
"\n"
"The books on the left, the table of contents on the right: **Entry** (as in "
"the book), **New** (suggestion or own name), **Note**. Below are the first "
"lines of book text at the selected entry.\n"
"\n"
"The table of contents is a tree: sub-entries are indented below their "
"parent, the small arrow in front folds them in and out.\n"
"\n"
"**Names**\n"
"- **Double-click** on a row (or F2) edits the new name. Leave it empty or "
"press **Del** = original.\n"
"- **Apply** (Ctrl+Enter) applies what is in the New column - including the "
"unchanged suggestions.\n"
"- **Apply all** applies the suggestions of all books that have no "
"questionable entries and are not being edited.\n"
"- **Suggestions** resets the book to the suggestions, **Original** to the "
"unchanged table of contents (including the levels).\n"
"\n"
"Where suggestions come from: the lines below a chapter number in the book "
"text (how many: [[einst_kapitel]]).\n"
"\n"
"**More**\n"
"- [[suche_filter]]\n"
"- [[kapitel_ebenen]]\n"
"- [[kapitel_fragwuerdig]]\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:314
msgid "Changing levels"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:314
msgid ""
"\n"
"- Select entries (several with Ctrl- or Shift-click), then **Tab** or "
"**Alt+→** (or **Down** in the toolbar) moves them one level down, "
"**Shift+Tab** or **Alt+←** (or **Up**) one level up.\n"
"- Sub-entries move along, as in Word's outline view.\n"
"- An entry can be at most one level deeper than the one above; the first "
"one is always at the top. What is not possible is said in the status bar.\n"
"- Column Note: \"Level 2 → 3\". Apply and save as with the names.\n"
"- When saving, the nesting in nav.xhtml and toc.ncx is rebuilt; the entries "
"themselves (links, names, IDs) stay unchanged, reading positions in "
"KOReader stay valid. Bookends (%chap_title_1 etc.) follows it.\n"
"- If nav.xhtml and toc.ncx have a different number of entries, the levels "
"of this book cannot be changed; the status bar gives the reason.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:322
msgid "Questionable names and decisions"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:322
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Questionable** (red background, ⚠ in the list) are:\n"
"- the same name several times in the book,\n"
"- very short or very long names,\n"
"- chapters without names between chapters with names,\n"
"- books with only a few matches.\n"
"A click on the row shows the reason in the status bar. Apply the name, "
"change it or put the original back - after applying, the entry no longer "
"counts as questionable.\n"
"\n"
"**Yellowish** rows have only a number and nothing was found in the text; a "
"double-click lets you enter your own name.\n"
"\n"
"**Decisions**\n"
"Applied names are remembered in **tocfix_entscheidungen.json** in the "
"settings folder ([[dateien]]). If the same book turns up again (another "
"copy, after a new transfer from Calibre), these names are suggested at "
"once.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:335
msgid ""
"\n"
"The books on the left, in the middle a form with the metadata stored in the "
"book: **title**, **authors**, **author sort**, **series** and **number**, "
"**publisher**, **published**, **language**, **ISBN** and **tags**, followed "
"by your **own fields** from Calibre columns ([[metadaten_felder]]). Below "
"the form the **blurb** ([[klappentexte]]), on the right the **cover** "
"([[cover]]).\n"
"\n"
"Several books at once: select them with Ctrl+click or Shift+click in the "
"list ([[metadaten_mehrere]]).\n"
"\n"
"- Type in the fields; the book turns red (changed, not applied). **Apply** "
"(Ctrl+Enter or Enter in a field) applies form and blurb and makes the book "
"yellow, **Save** (Ctrl+S) writes it into the file, with backup.\n"
"- **Discard** restores values, blurb and cover from the file. This also "
"drops a series taken over with **Series and blurbs from Calibre** in the "
"library.\n"
"- Several authors are separated with **&**, as in Calibre: \"Dan Abnett & "
"Graham McNeill\". The **author sort** works the same way (\"Abnett, Dan & "
"McNeill, Graham\"); with as many names as authors each author gets his own, "
"otherwise the first one gets the whole text.\n"
"- **Number** sits behind the series and only takes numbers; the arrows (or "
"arrow up/down, mouse wheel) count up and down. It may have decimals, e.g. "
"1.5 or 0.9 for a story between two books; a comma is turned into a point, "
"as in Calibre.\n"
"- **to** behind the number is only for a **collection** (omnibus): an "
"omnibus of books 1 to 3 gets number 1 and to 3; leave it empty for a single "
"book. Calibre and KOReader only know one number, so they see 1 and sort the "
"omnibus where book 1 would be. Bookbench keeps the last number in the book "
"itself (an extra entry Calibre and KOReader leave alone), shows \"1–3\" in "
"the library and counts 2 and 3 as present in [[reihen]]. **Number series** "
"keeps the range: after an omnibus 1–3 the next book gets 4.\n"
"- **Tags** are separated with commas.\n"
"- **Suggestions:** authors, series, publisher, language, tags and own text "
"fields suggest values from the other books while you type; **arrow down** "
"in an empty field shows all. With several authors or tags the suggestion is "
"for the part after the last & or comma.\n"
"- **Language** as a code, e.g. en, de or en-GB. KOReader uses it for "
"hyphenation.\n"
"- **Published** is the publication date (dc:date in the book, where Calibre "
"and KOReader read it): 2015, 2015-03 or 2015-03-01; 1.3.2015 or 3.2015 are "
"turned into that form when applying. A year and a full date of the same "
"year count as equal when comparing with Calibre. Can be switched off in "
"[[einst_metadaten]].\n"
"- Below the form Bookbench warns about an invalid ISBN, an impossible date, "
"a missing language or title, a number without series and values that do not "
"fit an own field (e.g. letters in a number column).\n"
"- If Calibre or KOReader have a different value, it is shown behind the "
"field (below it when there is not enough room): **← Calibre: …**. A click "
"on ← puts it into the field ([[metadaten_calibre]]). This needs Calibre's "
"metadata.calibre next to the books; without it there is nothing to compare, "
"and the one-time import ([[calibre_import]]) is the way to take Calibre's "
"values.\n"
"\n"
"The cover panel on the right can be made wider or narrower with the divider "
"between form and cover; the width is remembered, a double-click on the "
"divider restores the default.\n"
"\n"
"The file name is never changed: KOReader keeps reading positions and "
"highlights in a folder named after the file (.sdr), they would be lost.\n"
"\n"
"After saving, KOReader only shows the new values after re-reading the book "
"information (Cover browser > Extract and cache book information).\n"
"\n"
"**Toolbar**: Apply, Discard, **Look up …** ([[metadaten_nachschlagen]]), "
"From Calibre (only with the Calibre comparison on), **Complete ▾** for all "
"books: look up all missing blurbs, look up all missing covers, import from "
"a Calibre library ([[calibre_import]]), fill metadata gaps from Calibre; "
"**Several books ▾** for the selected books ([[metadaten_mehrere]]).\n"
"\n"
"**Markers** in the list show what a book lacks: speech bubble (blurb), "
"picture (cover), not-equal sign (Calibre), label (language/ISBN) - see "
"[[status]].\n"
"\n"
"**More**\n"
"- [[metadaten_nachschlagen]] - looking up metadata, blurb and cover\n"
"- [[metadaten_mehrere]] - editing several books at once\n"
"- [[metadaten_calibre]] - comparing with Calibre and KOReader\n"
"- [[calibre_import]] - taking Calibre's data over once\n"
"- [[metadaten_felder]]\n"
"- [[klappentexte]]\n"
"- [[cover]]\n"
"- [[suche_filter]]\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:372
msgid "Looking up metadata"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:372
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Look up …** in the toolbar of the Metadata area (also Edit > Look up "
"metadata … and right-click in the library) asks Google Books and Open "
"Library for the selected book and shows what they know, like Calibre's "
"\"Download metadata\". Free; an own Google Books key avoids the shared "
"quota ([[einst_online]]).\n"
"\n"
"**Search**\n"
"- Title, author and ISBN come from the book and can be changed; Enter or "
"**Search** searches again.\n"
"- **Language**: \"Same as the book\" (or the preferred language from "
"[[einst_klappentexte]]). Another language searches without the ISBN - an "
"ISBN belongs to exactly one edition, the one of your book - and only finds "
"editions in that language. Their title may be a translation: type it into "
"the title field if nothing turns up.\n"
"\n"
"**Hits** (left) with a small cover, title, author, publisher, date, "
"language and source. Open Library often has several editions under one "
"work; its hits come without ISBN for that reason.\n"
"\n"
"**Comparison** (right): one row per field - Now (the book) and Hit - plus "
"subtitle (if you have an own field like #subtitle, [[metadaten_felder]]), "
"blurb (whole text as tooltip) and cover (both images).\n"
"- Ticked at first are only fields the book does not have. Values the book "
"already has are only replaced if you tick them; equal values show (=) and "
"cannot be ticked.\n"
"- A click on a row ticks or unticks it. **Open the hit's page** shows the "
"source in the browser.\n"
"- Tags from Google Books are its categories (\"Fiction / Science Fiction\" "
"becomes Fiction, Science Fiction).\n"
"\n"
"**Apply** puts the ticked values into the book like the form does (yellow), "
"the blurb as applied and the cover through the cover panel (format and size "
"as set in [[einst_cover]]). **Save** (Ctrl+S) writes them with backup.\n"
"\n"
"**Several books** selected ([[metadaten_mehrere]]): the window goes through "
"them one after another - **Apply and next** (Ctrl+Enter) or **Skip**. After "
"the last book it closes; the status bar says how many were changed.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:390
msgid "Several books at once"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:390
msgid ""
"\n"
"Select several books in the list on the left: **Ctrl+click** adds or "
"removes a book, **Shift+click** selects a range, **Ctrl+A** (or Several "
"books ▾ > Select all books in the list) selects everything the search and "
"filter show. In the library, select books the same way and right-click > "
"**Edit metadata of n books**.\n"
"\n"
"**The form with several books**\n"
"- A field shows the value all selected books share. Where they differ it "
"stays empty with **(various)** in grey.\n"
"- Only fields you **type into** count; their label turns red. A field you "
"empty is cleared in all books. Fields you do not touch keep each book's own "
"value - so you can set the publisher of 20 books without touching their "
"titles.\n"
"- **Authors**, **tags** and own list fields have a choice behind them: "
"**Replace** (the field gets exactly what you typed), **Add** (your values "
"are added, nothing twice) or **Remove** (your values are taken out, "
"upper/lower case does not matter). Separate several values with & (authors) "
"or a comma (tags).\n"
"- **Apply** (Ctrl+Enter) first shows a **preview**: every book with the "
"fields that change, before and after, and warnings (e.g. an impossible "
"date). Apply there puts the values into the books like the form (yellow); "
"**Save** (Ctrl+S) writes them, with a backup of each file.\n"
"- **Discard** asks, then restores metadata, blurb and cover of all selected "
"books that have changes.\n"
"- Blurb and cover stay empty while several books are selected; they are "
"edited one book at a time.\n"
"\n"
"**Several books ▾** (toolbar)\n"
"- **Number series …**: numbers the selected books in the order of the list, "
"from a start value with a step (0.5 gives 1, 1.5, 2 …). Optionally gives "
"them all the same series; empty keeps each book's series. Sort or filter "
"the list first if the order is not right.\n"
"- **Search and replace …**: in one field of the selected books (with one "
"book selected: only that one). Without **regular expression** the text is "
"taken literally; with it, \\1 in the replacement inserts the first group. "
"**Match case** is off by default. The window counts the books with a "
"match.\n"
"- **Author sort from authors**: sets the author sort the way Calibre does "
"by default (\"Dan Abnett & Nick Kyme\" → \"Abnett, Dan & Kyme, Nick\").\n"
"- All three show the preview first, like Apply.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:407
msgid "Calibre and KOReader"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:407
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Calibre**\n"
"If Calibre's **metadata.calibre** is found ([[bibliothek_calibre]]) and the "
"comparison is on ([[einst_metadaten]]), Calibre's value is shown next to "
"(or below) every field where it differs: **← Calibre: …**. A click on **←** "
"puts the value into the field. Matching values are not shown.\n"
"\n"
"- **From Calibre** (toolbar) puts all differing values of this book into "
"the form - check them, then apply.\n"
"- **Complete ▾ > Fill metadata gaps from Calibre** (also in the Edit menu "
"of the library) works on all books, but only on fields that are empty in "
"the book. It applies directly (yellow); save afterwards.\n"
"- A **not-equal sign** in the list means title, authors, series or number "
"differ from Calibre. The filter **Differs from Calibre** also counts the "
"other fields.\n"
"- Calibre stores languages as three letters (eng); Bookbench compares them "
"with the two-letter codes of the book (en) and takes over the short form.\n"
"\n"
"**Important:** When Calibre sends a book to the e-reader again, it writes "
"its own values into the file. Change the metadata in Calibre as well, "
"otherwise your changes in Bookbench are overwritten. If you no longer use "
"Calibre, switch the comparison off: [[einst_metadaten]].\n"
"\n"
"**KOReader**\n"
"If you edited the book information in KOReader itself (Book information > "
"long-press a field), KOReader keeps it in **custom_metadata.lua** in the "
".sdr folder next to the book and shows it **instead of** the values in the "
"file. Bookbench shows these values next to the fields as **← KOReader: …** "
"(only when the folder with the .sdr folders is opened, e.g. the e-reader "
"directly) and points it out below the form. The filter **Edited in "
"KOReader** lists these books. To make KOReader show the file again, reset "
"the fields in KOReader.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:421 settings.py:688
msgid "Own fields"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:421
msgid ""
"\n"
"In Calibre you can add columns of your own (Preferences > Add your own "
"columns), e.g. a subtitle with the lookup name **#subtitle**. Bookbench "
"shows such columns as further fields below the usual ones - which ones and "
"with what label, you decide in [[einst_metadaten]]. The label is yours and "
"is not translated: a column named \"Untertitel\" in Calibre stays "
"\"Untertitel\" in the English interface until you rename it there. **From "
"Calibre** there adds all columns found in metadata.calibre at once.\n"
"\n"
"- Bookbench reads and writes the columns the way Calibre stores them in the "
"book: in EPUB 2 one entry per column, in EPUB 3 all own columns together in "
"one entry. Columns you do not show stay untouched.\n"
"- If the book does not have a column yet, Bookbench takes its definition "
"(type, how values are separated) from metadata.calibre, so Calibre "
"recognises it when adding the book again. Without metadata.calibre a plain "
"text column is written.\n"
"- Columns with several values (\"Ampersand separated text\", \"Comma "
"separated text\") are shown joined with \" & \" or \", \". Careful: a text "
"containing **&** is then split into two values, just like in Calibre.\n"
"- Number columns (whole numbers, decimals, rating) only take numbers, "
"yes/no columns yes or no; dates are shown without time.\n"
"- KOReader only knows title, authors, series, language, tags and "
"description; own fields are for Calibre and for you.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:430
msgid "Import from Calibre"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:430
msgid ""
"\n"
"If you want to stop using Calibre, take its metadata over once into the "
"books: **File > Import from Calibre library …** (also in **Complete ▾** in "
"the Metadata area).\n"
"\n"
"1. Choose the folder of your Calibre library, the one with **metadata.db** "
"(in Calibre: right-click the library button > Show folder). Bookbench "
"remembers it. The database is only read, so Calibre may stay open.\n"
"2. Bookbench matches the books of the opened folder with Calibre: by the "
"Calibre ID in the book (UUID), the ISBN, or title and first author. Only "
"unique matches count; books without a match are listed on the tab **Not "
"matched**.\n"
"3. The table shows every field where Calibre has a different value: book, "
"field, value in the book, value from Calibre. Untick rows you do not want "
"(click on the box or press Space). **Only empty fields** shows only fields "
"that are empty in the book. **Blurbs** adds Calibre's descriptions.\n"
"4. If Calibre has own columns (e.g. #subtitle) that are not fields yet, "
"**Add as own fields** adds them ([[metadaten_felder]]); then their values "
"appear in the table too.\n"
"5. **Apply ticked** applies the values (yellow), then save with Ctrl+S.\n"
"\n"
"Afterwards switch the Calibre comparison off ([[einst_metadaten]]) if "
"Calibre does not manage the books anymore. Calibre's own fields like date "
"added, rating stars or read status only come along as own columns.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:441
msgid ""
"\n"
"The blurb is below the form in the Metadata area. The text can be edited "
"directly; the divider between form and blurb can be dragged (a double-click "
"puts it back below the form). If the form no longer fits above it, it gets "
"a scrollbar (the mouse wheel scrolls it too).\n"
"\n"
"- Books without a blurb have a **speech bubble** in the list. If Calibre "
"has a description for the book, the status bar says so when the mouse is "
"over it; the filter **Blurb only in Calibre** lists these books.\n"
"- **Apply** (Ctrl+Enter) applies the text together with the form, "
"**Discard** restores both from the file. An empty field applied = remove "
"the blurb.\n"
"- **Look up** and **From Calibre** are small buttons above the text; "
"**Complete ▾** in the toolbar looks up all missing blurbs. The window "
"**Look up …** (toolbar) shows the blurbs of several editions to choose "
"from, also in another language ([[metadaten_nachschlagen]]).\n"
"- Below the text: where it comes from. This is also saved in the EPUB (meta "
"bookbench:blurb-source) and shown in the Blurb column of the library.\n"
"\n"
"After saving, re-read the book information in KOReader (Cover browser > "
"Extract and cache book information), otherwise Bookshelf still shows the "
"old state.\n"
"\n"
"**More**\n"
"- [[suche_filter]]\n"
"- [[klappentexte_nachschlagen]]\n"
"- [[klappentexte_profile]]\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:456
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Look up** (one book, above the blurb) and **Complete ▾ > Look up all "
"missing blurbs** (all without a blurb, in the background) go through these "
"steps until one has a text:\n"
"- **Google Books**, then **Open Library**: by ISBN, otherwise by title and "
"author; the edition in the book's language is preferred. Free; for Google "
"Books an own key is optional ([[kosten]]).\n"
"- **Language**: normally the book's own (dc:language). With another "
"**preferred language** ([[einst_klappentexte]]), e.g. German for your "
"English books, the search goes without the ISBN (it belongs to the English "
"edition) and only finds editions in that language; Claude is told to take "
"the blurb of that edition. Works best where the title is the same in both "
"languages (Eisenhorn); for a translated title use the window Look up … and "
"type the title there ([[metadaten_nachschlagen]]).\n"
"- **Claude** with the first source profile that fits the book "
"([[klappentexte_profile]]): fetches the pages of the profile and copies the "
"publisher blurb word for word. About 1 to 2 cents.\n"
"\n"
"A text that is too short or too long is not taken (limits: "
"[[einst_klappentexte]]). What was found is not applied at once: it is in "
"the text field (red) until you apply it.\n"
"\n"
"**Look up all**\n"
"- Skips books for which Calibre has a description - **From Calibre** is "
"there for those, and it costs nothing.\n"
"- Asks before starting if Claude could be needed, with the maximum cost.\n"
"- Without an API key only the free sources are asked, without an error "
"message.\n"
"\n"
"**When nothing is found**\n"
"The message at the end says per source why, and below the blurb the result "
"of the last look-up stays as long as the book has no text:\n"
"- **daily quota used up (429)**: Google Books without a key shares one "
"daily quota with everyone else and often answers like this. An own key "
"([[einst_online]]) gives you your own quota.\n"
"- **book found, but no blurb stored**: the source knows the book but has no "
"description (Open Library often).\n"
"- **book not found**: no matching title and author (or ISBN).\n"
"A quota or network error is not remembered as tried; the next look-up asks "
"that source again.\n"
"\n"
"**Cache**\n"
"What was found and what was already tried is in **blurbs_cache.json** "
"([[dateien]]). Looking up again costs nothing. Another preferred language "
"has entries of its own, so an English hit does not stop the German look-up. "
"If a profile is changed, Claude tries again for the books it covers; the "
"free sources are asked again only after a network error. The cache can be "
"emptied: [[einst_erweitert]].\n"
"\n"
"Claude needs an API key and the package **anthropic** (pip install "
"anthropic): [[einst_online]].\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:481 help_topics.py:1121 settings.py:56 settings.py:508
msgid "Source profiles"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:481
msgid ""
"\n"
"A source profile tells Claude where to look for a blurb. They are set up "
"under Settings > Books > Blurbs > Source profiles "
"([[einst_klappentexte_profile]]). The **first active profile that fits** "
"the book is used.\n"
"\n"
"**Fields**\n"
"- **Applies to**: parts of the publisher, series or author, separated by "
"commas, case does not matter; one match is enough. All empty: every book.\n"
"- **Pages to fetch**: addresses with placeholders, one per line, fetched in "
"this order, e.g. https://stormlightarchive.fandom.com/wiki/{title}.\n"
"- **Placeholders**: {title}, {author}, {series} become wiki page names "
"(spaces as _); {title_q} and {author_q} are for search addresses (?q=…); "
"{base} is the title without \"Omnibus\" (lines with {base} are skipped for "
"other books, lines with {series} for books without a series). Lines with "
"unknown placeholders are skipped.\n"
"- **Further allowed sites**: Claude may only fetch pages from these and the "
"sites of the addresses above.\n"
"- **Section with the blurb**: what the section is usually called there "
"(\"Official Description\"). A hint for Claude.\n"
"- **Topic**: e.g. \"Star Wars\"; a hint for Claude.\n"
"- **Web search**: if no page fits, search once in the allowed sites; a "
"profile without any sites searches the whole web (publisher pages first, "
"then big bookshops) and may search twice.\n"
"\n"
"**Included**\n"
"- **Lexicanum (Warhammer 40,000)**: for Black Library / Games Workshop and "
"the Warhammer series. Lexicanum, then the Fandom wiki.\n"
"- **Web search**: for all other books.\n"
"Both can be changed, switched off or deleted; **Defaults** brings them "
"back.\n"
"\n"
"**Tips**\n"
"- A fan wiki for a series (\"Fandom\") usually has the blurb: one line with "
"{title}, section \"Official Description\".\n"
"- Put specific profiles above general ones; the web search profile belongs "
"at the bottom.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:502
msgid ""
"\n"
"The cover is shown right of the form, as large as there is room. **Click on "
"the cover** (also the small one in Table of contents) shows it as large as "
"the screen allows; a second click on the cover, a click into the window or "
"Esc closes it. Choosing another book closes it too. A right-click opens a "
"menu with all cover actions.\n"
"\n"
"- Below the cover: size in pixels, format, file size and where it is in the "
"EPUB.\n"
"- **From file …**, **Look up**, **Export …** (saves the cover shown as an "
"image file) and **Discard new** are below the cover; **Complete ▾ > Look up "
"all missing covers** is in the toolbar.\n"
"- As long as a new cover is not saved, the old one and the new one are "
"shown next to or below each other.\n"
"- Books without a cover have a **picture**, covers lower than the limit a "
"**small picture** in the list ([[einst_cover]]); the filters **No cover** "
"and **Small cover** list them.\n"
"\n"
"After saving, re-read the book information in KOReader (Cover browser > "
"Extract and cache book information), otherwise the cover browser still "
"shows the old cover.\n"
"\n"
"**More**\n"
"- [[cover_aendern]]\n"
"- [[cover_bild]]\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:516
msgid "Changing and looking up covers"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:516
msgid ""
"\n"
"- **From file** takes an image file (JPEG, PNG, GIF, WebP).\n"
"- **Look up** searches Open Library and Google Books (free, no key) by "
"ISBN, title and author and shows what was found side by side, with size; a "
"click chooses, OK takes it, a double-click takes it at once. Tiny preview "
"images are left out.\n"
"- **Complete ▾ > Look up all missing covers** does that in the background "
"for all books without a cover and takes the largest image found. Check them "
"afterwards.\n"
"- **Discard** drops the new cover. **Ctrl+S** saves, with a backup as "
"always.\n"
"\n"
"The sources, the smallest height that counts and how many to choose from: "
"[[einst_cover_nachschlagen]].\n"
"\n"
"**Tips**\n"
"- Open Library often has large scans, sometimes of another edition (another "
"cover). Google Books has the publisher's image, usually smaller.\n"
"- The Edit menu has the same actions while Metadata is shown; **Look up all "
"missing covers** also in the library.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:528
msgid "What happens to the image"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:528
msgid ""
"\n"
"- The new cover keeps the **format** and the **file name** of the old one "
"(a PNG becomes a JPEG if the old cover is a JPEG). That way the manifest "
"and a title page that shows the image stay valid.\n"
"- It is shrunk only if it is higher than the limit in the settings (0 = "
"never); it is never enlarged ([[einst_cover]]). If nothing has to be "
"converted or shrunk, the file goes into the book unchanged, byte for byte.\n"
"- A book without a cover gets cover-bookbench.jpg next to the content.opf, "
"an entry in the manifest (EPUB 3: properties=\"cover-image\") and <meta "
"name=\"cover\"> (EPUB 2; KOReader reads both).\n"
"- Converting and shrinking need **Pillow** (pip install pillow); showing "
"works without it. Without Pillow only an image in the right format can be "
"inserted unchanged.\n"
"- Transparent areas become white when converting to JPEG.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:535
msgid ""
"\n"
"All series of the opened folder in one list, each with its books below it "
"(click the arrow or **Expand all**). Books in the same series are found by "
"the series name; upper/lower case and extra spaces do not matter.\n"
"\n"
"**Columns of a series**\n"
"- **No.**: lowest and highest number.\n"
"- **Author**: the authors of its books (all of them in the tooltip).\n"
"- **Read status**: how many books are finished, from KOReader's reading "
"state ([[bibliothek_lesestand]]). Empty as long as Bookbench knows nothing "
"about your reading.\n"
"- **Next**: the book you are reading (the one with the lowest number if "
"several), otherwise the first unread one. Books on hold are skipped. In the "
"list of books it is in **bold**.\n"
"- **Missing**: missing numbers, numbers that appear twice and books without "
"a number.\n"
"\n"
"**Missing numbers** are the whole numbers from 1 to the highest number that "
"no book has. They are shown in grey between the books where they are "
"missing (e.g. \"missing (4)\" at 3–6). Numbers with decimals like 0.9 or "
"1.5 (novellas, short stories between two novels) never count as missing, "
"and they do not fill a gap either: with 0.9, 1.5 and 2, number 1 is "
"missing. A series that starts at 0 is fine. Series numbered above 999 "
"(years and the like) are not checked. A series with missing numbers gets "
"the gap pictogram, in the list and in the sidebar ([[status]]).\n"
"\n"
"**Search and filter**: the search looks in the series name and in titles "
"and authors of its books. Filters: All series, With gaps, Numbers to check "
"(duplicates, books without number), Started not finished, Not started, "
"Finished, and **Books without series** (a plain list of the books that have "
"no series yet).\n"
"\n"
"**Commands**\n"
"- **Number series …** (toolbar, or right-click on a series): selects the "
"books of the series in the [[metadaten]] area in the order of their numbers "
"and opens Number series there ([[metadaten_mehrere]]). After the preview "
"the new numbers are applied (yellow) and shown here right away; **Save** "
"(Ctrl+S) writes them.\n"
"- Right-click on a series: **Edit metadata of n books** (all books of the "
"series in the Metadata area), **Show series in Library** (library search "
"set to the series name), **Move whole series …** (rename it, or merge it "
"into another), **Collection from series** (a collection with its books in "
"number order, [[sammlungen]]) and **Add to collection**.\n"
"- **Move to series …** (right-click on books, several with Ctrl+click or "
"Shift+click; also in the library): choose a series from the list or type a "
"new one, and what happens to the numbers - keep them, add the books at the "
"end of the series (after its highest number) or number them from a start "
"value. A collection (omnibus 1–3) keeps its range. An empty series removes "
"series and number. The preview shows every change first; Save (Ctrl+S) "
"writes it.\n"
"- Double-click (or Enter) on a book opens it like in the library "
"([[einst_oberflaeche]]); right-click offers the other places and **Show in "
"Library**. In the library, right-click > **Show series** jumps here.\n"
"\n"
"**Collections (omnibus)**: a book with a range in Metadata (number 1, to 3) "
"covers 1, 2 and 3. It shows \"1–3\", and below it the books it contains, "
"named after the top entries of its table of contents when there are enough "
"of them (otherwise \"in this collection\"). Having book 2 on its own as "
"well does not count as a duplicate.\n"
"\n"
"**Series of collections**: collections with a series "
"([[sammlungen_metadaten]], e.g. 21 omnibuses) appear here too, with \"n "
"collections\" in the Author column. Their collections stand below by "
"number, missing numbers in grey as for books, and the read status counts "
"the books (and stories) of each collection. A double-click or Enter opens a "
"collection in the Collections area; right-click > Show in Collections.\n"
"\n"
"Series and numbers that are applied but not yet saved already count here.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:561
msgid ""
"\n"
"KOReader's **collections** are your own lists of books: Favorites, \"To "
"read\", a custom omnibus. KOReader shows them in the file manager "
"(Collections) and Bookshelf can show them as shelves. A book can be in "
"several collections; it stays where it is, a collection only points to it.\n"
"\n"
"**Where they are saved**\n"
"- In a **library on this computer** ([[bibliothek_rechner]]) in its "
".bookbench/library.json. The sync in the E-reader area takes them to the "
"e-reader and brings changes made there back ([[kobo_abgleich]]).\n"
"- With the **e-reader** opened directly: in KOReader's "
"settings/collection.lua on the e-reader, backed up before every change. "
"KOReader reads it when it starts next.\n"
"- In any other folder there are no collections.\n"
"Every change is saved at once (no Save needed).\n"
"\n"
"**On the left** the collections, Favorites first. **New …**, **Rename …**, "
"**Delete** (the books stay; Favorites cannot be renamed or deleted).\n"
"\n"
"**On the right** the entries of the selected collection in reading order, "
"with series and read status. Drag entries to another place, or use **Move "
"up** / **Move down**. **Add books …** chooses books from the library "
"(several with Ctrl+click), **Remove** (or Del) takes entries out of the "
"collection. **Sort by series number** sorts by series and number. A "
"double-click opens a book. Once Bookbench has set the order, KOReader shows "
"the collection sorted by hand, in that order.\n"
"\n"
"**Paste reading order …** (toolbar) creates a collection from a list, e.g. "
"the reading order of an omnibus on heresyomnibus.com: copy the list from "
"the page and paste it. One entry per line like \"2. The Wolf of Ash and "
"Fire (EoT) by Graham McNeill\"; number, the short name of an anthology in "
"brackets and \"by …\" are optional, also on lines of their own. Each line "
"is matched to a book of the library by title (a series before a colon, "
"upper/lower case and punctuation do not matter) and author; the window "
"shows the result at once.\n"
"- Lines with the short name of an anthology are **stories in an "
"anthology**: Bookbench looks for the title in the tables of contents of all "
"books and makes it a story entry (see below).\n"
"- What is not found becomes a grey **placeholder** that keeps its place. "
"**Choose …** replaces it with a book, or for a story with an entry of an "
"anthology. Placeholders are not written to the e-reader, but the numbering "
"there leaves their place free.\n"
"\n"
"**Series, blurb and cover** of a collection ([[sammlungen_metadaten]]): the "
"fields above the entries.\n"
"\n"
"**Stories in anthologies** ([[sammlungen_geschichten]]): **Add story …** "
"picks a book and an entry of its table of contents. The story then is an "
"entry of the collection like a book.\n"
"\n"
"**From other areas**: right-click in the library or in Book series > **Add "
"to collection** (an existing one or a new one); in Book series also "
"**Collection from series**.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:584
msgid "Series, blurb and cover of a collection"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:584
msgid ""
"\n"
"KOReader itself only knows the name of a collection. Bookbench adds a "
"**series** with a **number**, a **blurb** and a **cover** - e.g. 21 "
"omnibuses as the series \"Horus Heresy Omnibus\", #1 to #21. KOReader shows "
"them with Bookbench's patch **2-bookbench-collections** on the e-reader.\n"
"\n"
"**In Bookbench** (Collections area, above the entries of the selected "
"collection):\n"
"- **Series** (pick one of the series of your other collections or type a "
"new one) and **Number** (decimals allowed). **Blurb**: any text. **Apply** "
"saves the three. The divider between these fields and the entries can be "
"dragged.\n"
"- **Cover** on the right, used like the cover in Metadata: a click shows it "
"as large as the screen allows (a second click closes it), right-click opens "
"the menu, a picture can be dropped onto it. Below: size and format, **From "
"file …** (JPG or PNG), **From a book …** (the cover of a book of the "
"collection), **Export …**, **Remove cover**. There is no looking up (a "
"collection you made yourself is nowhere online), and the cover is saved at "
"once, like everything in collections. The panel can be made wider or "
"narrower with the divider. The picture is stored in the library "
"(.bookbench/covers) or on the e-reader (settings/bookbench_covers); the "
"sync copies it along.\n"
"- The list of collections shows the series and number after the name.\n"
"\n"
"**In KOReader** (with the patch):\n"
"- In the list of collections a **series is one entry**, with a folder "
"symbol and the number of its collections. Tapping it shows its collections, "
"sorted by number (shown on the right). The return arrow at the bottom goes "
"back to all collections; coming back from a collection of a series lands in "
"its series again.\n"
"- A collection of a series shows \"Series · #3\" under its title.\n"
"- **About this collection**: long-press a collection in the list, or the "
"menu button (top left) inside the collection. Shows the cover, the name, "
"series and number and the blurb; a tap closes it. **Collection options …** "
"leads to KOReader's own menu.\n"
"- Collections without series, blurb or cover look as usual.\n"
"\n"
"On the e-reader: settings/bookbench_collections.lua (written by Bookbench) "
"and the pictures in settings/bookbench_covers.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:600
msgid "Stories in anthologies"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:600
msgid ""
"\n"
"A KOReader collection can only hold whole files. With Bookbench's patch "
"**2-bookbench-collections** on the e-reader it can also hold a story inside "
"an anthology - without cutting the story out into a file of its own.\n"
"\n"
"**In Bookbench**: **Add story …** in the Collections area. On the left the "
"books (the search also looks into their tables of contents), on the right "
"the table of contents of the selected book. One entry = the story runs to "
"the next entry of the same or a higher level; several entries in a row = a "
"story with parts, from the first to the last. The title is that of the "
"entry, it can be changed. Stories from a pasted reading order are found by "
"title. Entries are matched in KOReader by their title (and which one it is, "
"if the title appears more than once), so **save** changed chapter names "
"before syncing.\n"
"\n"
"**On the e-reader** (patch needed - **Install patch** in the Collections "
"area when the e-reader is opened directly, or Preview > Patches; it also "
"removes the older 2-bookbench-stories):\n"
"- The collection shows the story at its place, with \"in <anthology>\" and "
"✓ or a percentage once it was read.\n"
"- Tapping opens the anthology right at the start of the story, or where you "
"stopped. The story has a **reading position of its own**: the anthology "
"keeps its own (KOReader's progress of the anthology is put back when you "
"leave the story).\n"
"- At the **end of the story** (when you turn past its last page) a window "
"offers: **Next: …** (the next entry of the collection, book or story), "
"**Back to \"collection\"**, **Read on in this book** (leave the story and "
"go on in the anthology from here) and **Back to the last page**.\n"
"- **Long-press** a story: open it, open the book normally, mark it read or "
"unread.\n"
"- Arranging a collection by hand in KOReader keeps the stories at their new "
"place until Bookbench writes the collection anew.\n"
"\n"
"**Reading state**: the patch notes progress and \"read\" per story in "
"settings/bookbench_stories_state.lua on the e-reader. Bookbench shows it in "
"the Collections area when the e-reader is opened directly.\n"
"\n"
"**Limits**: only EPUB (reflowable books). The time KOReader's statistics "
"count goes to the anthology. Bookshelf's collection shelves show only the "
"books, not the stories.\n"
"\n"
"The files on the e-reader: settings/collection.lua (the books, as KOReader "
"knows them), settings/bookbench_stories.lua (the stories, written by "
"Bookbench), settings/bookbench_stories_state.lua (written by the patch).\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:618
msgid ""
"\n"
"Your own dictionaries for KOReader: read, edit, fill missing terms, build "
"from a wiki, build the files. The dictionary is chosen at the top; "
"Bookbench remembers all that were ever opened or created.\n"
"\n"
"**Types**\n"
"- **from a wiki**: a database like the one of wikidict.py (wikidict.sqlite "
"in the working folder; wh40kdict.sqlite from earlier versions is still "
"recognized). Entries come from wiki articles ([[woerterbuch_aufbau]]), plus "
"your own.\n"
"- **own entries**: a database without a wiki; entries by hand or from "
"Claude, missing terms from the lookup history and from the books (frequent "
"names).\n"
"- **StarDict folder**: a finished dictionary without a database, e.g. a "
"copy on the go, one from the e-reader or from the web. That is enough for "
"reading and editing.\n"
"\n"
"**New …** creates a dictionary (working folder, name in KOReader, file "
"name, topic for Claude, for wiki dictionaries the wiki address and "
"optionally a second site for cross-checking). **Open …** takes a working "
"folder (with wikidict.sqlite) or a folder with an .ifo file; it also "
"searches below (also under .adds), so e.g. the root of an e-reader backup "
"is enough. With several matches you get a choice. **Properties …** changes "
"name, topic, spoilers and the books filter. **Remove from list** only takes "
"it out of the list, the files stay.\n"
"\n"
"**More**\n"
"- [[woerterbuch_anzeigen]]\n"
"- [[woerterbuch_bearbeiten]]\n"
"- [[woerterbuch_aufbau]]\n"
"- [[woerterbuch_unterwegs]]\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:634
msgid "Views and missing terms"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:634
msgid ""
"\n"
"- **Entries**: everything in the dictionary. The search also finds other "
"names (\"Strider\" finds Aragorn).\n"
"- **Missing terms**: names from the books without an entry (from the step "
"Search books) and **words that were looked up in KOReader and are missing** "
"(dictionary pictogram in the list, first). The lookup history comes from "
"the connected e-reader, otherwise from the newest backup of the KOReader "
"settings ([[kobo_sicherungen]]). Everyday words in lower case are left "
"out.\n"
"- Which looked-up words belong to which dictionary is decided by the "
"searched books and the **books filter** in the properties (parts of title, "
"series or author). Without either, all of them belong.\n"
"- **Ignored and removed**: terms that no longer count as missing, and "
"entries taken out; **Remove** or **Bring back** brings them back.\n"
"- **Steps** (only with a database): [[woerterbuch_aufbau]].\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:641
msgid "Editing entries and Claude"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:641
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Editing**\n"
"- Form: name, category, short description, other names (comma-separated; "
"KOReader also finds the entry under these), sections (a line with **#** is "
"the heading, the text below it). Below that the preview of how the entry "
"looks in KOReader.\n"
"- Entries from other dictionaries that do not have this structure are "
"edited as **HTML** or **text**.\n"
"- **Apply** (Ctrl+Enter) saves your own version (yellow), **Discard** "
"undoes changes in the form (red = not applied). Own versions always win, "
"even if things are summarized again later. **Discard own version "
"(original)** (Dictionary menu) discards them. **New** adds a term by hand.\n"
"\n"
"**Claude ▾** (for the chosen term; the result is always a draft to check; "
"topic and spoilers from the properties)\n"
"- **Search the web**: web search anywhere, text only from the pages found, "
"which are given as the source in the entry. About 2-4 cents.\n"
"- **Summarize from a page**: give an address, Claude reads only that page.\n"
"- **From own knowledge**: without a source; the entry says \"unverified\", "
"the list shows the dictionary pictogram.\n"
"- If Claude finds the full name (\"Baggins\" -> \"Bilbo Baggins\"), the "
"searched name becomes another name of the entry.\n"
"\n"
"Costs and what is sent: [[kosten]].\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:655
msgid "Steps and building"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:655
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Steps** (the steps of wikidict.py, with status, options and output; "
"**Cancel** stops at the next intermediate step, what was done so far stays "
"saved)\n"
"- **1. Load titles**: all article titles of the wiki.\n"
"- **2. Search books**: choose the books folder; finds the titles in the "
"books, names without an article become gaps. The book text never leaves the "
"computer.\n"
"- **3. Load articles**: only the ones that occur.\n"
"- **4. Summarize**: Claude writes the entries; batch costs half as much but "
"takes time (minutes to hours). The estimated cost is asked for confirmation "
"first.\n"
"- **5. Cross-check**: compare with the second site and fill gaps from "
"there.\n"
"- **6. Build**.\n"
"Dictionaries without a wiki only have Search books and Build. The packages "
"requests, mwparserfromhell, wordfreq and anthropic are needed.\n"
"\n"
"**Build** writes the dictionary files: for databases into the folder of the "
"last build (you are asked the first time), for StarDict folders into the "
"folder itself, backed up first (backup folder, subfolder Wörterbücher). "
"Then copy them to the e-reader ([[kobo_woerterbuecher]]) and restart "
"KOReader. Green = built since opening.\n"
"\n"
"wikidict.py on the command line also includes your own entries when "
"building.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:669
msgid "On the go without a database"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:669
msgid ""
"\n"
"Open the StarDict folder (e.g. the copy on the e-reader or on a stick) and "
"edit it. Bookbench records the changes in **bookbench.json** in this folder "
"(it does not go to the e-reader).\n"
"\n"
"Back home in the database: Properties > **Take changes from a StarDict "
"folder**, otherwise the next build overwrites them.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:674
msgid ""
"\n"
"Shows the connected e-reader with KOReader - Kobo, Kindle or PocketBook: "
"model, drive, firmware, KOReader version, free space.\n"
"\n"
"**Detection**\n"
"- Automatically every few seconds ([[einst_kobo]]), by the folders in the "
"root of a drive ([[kobo_geraete]]). Copies of the e-reader on the hard disk "
"(backups with a .kobo folder and the like) do not count, only the root of a "
"drive.\n"
"- Searched: on Linux /run/media, /media and /mnt, on Windows all drives; "
"also the opened books folder and the folders above it.\n"
"- With several connected e-readers a list appears next to the name.\n"
"\n"
"**Books: computer ↔ e-reader** (top): compares the library on this computer "
"with the e-reader and copies changes in both directions "
"([[kobo_abgleich]]).\n"
"\n"
"**Books** opens the whole e-reader in Library, Table of contents and "
"Metadata. Then metadata.calibre is found automatically too.\n"
"\n"
"**When connecting** Bookbench opens the books on the e-reader by itself if "
"no other folder is open; otherwise it asks. If the e-reader is linked with "
"a library on this computer, that library is opened instead and compared. "
"Can be changed: [[einst_kobo]].\n"
"\n"
"**More**\n"
"- [[kobo_abgleich]]\n"
"- [[kobo_geraete]]\n"
"- [[kobo_sicherungen]]\n"
"- [[kobo_woerterbuecher]]\n"
"- [[kobo_auswerfen]]\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:695
msgid "Syncing books"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:695
msgid ""
"\n"
"At the top of the E-reader area: the books of the library on this computer "
"([[bibliothek_rechner]]) compared with those on the connected e-reader. You "
"can edit on both sides; the sync brings them together.\n"
"\n"
"**Linking**\n"
"- **New library from e-reader** links the new library with this e-reader at "
"once.\n"
"- Otherwise **Link library …** chooses the folder. The link is remembered "
"per e-reader (serial number; Kindle/PocketBook: kind, model and drive name) "
"and noted in the library's library.json. **Other library …** changes it.\n"
"\n"
"**Reading state**\n"
"When the linked e-reader is connected, Bookbench also reads KOReader's "
"reading state (status, progress, reading time) and notes it in the library "
"([[bibliothek_lesestand]]). Nothing is written to the e-reader for that.\n"
"\n"
"**How changes are found**\n"
"After every sync Bookbench notes for each book its size and date on both "
"sides and a checksum of its content (.bookbench/abgleich.json in the "
"library folder). Next time each side is compared with that note, so it is "
"clear on which side a book changed. A different date alone does not count: "
"the e-reader's file system stores times in local time with a 2-second grid, "
"after the switch to summer time all files can look an hour off. The "
"checksum is only computed when size or date differ, so the e-reader is not "
"read completely every time.\n"
"\n"
"**The list** (only books with something to do)\n"
"- **new on the computer** → to the e-reader (checked). **only on the "
"e-reader** → to the computer (checked).\n"
"- **changed on the computer** / **changed on the e-reader**: copied to the "
"other side (checked).\n"
"- **changed on both sides**: *Leave as it is* by default; choose which side "
"wins. Below the list the differences: title, authors, series and other "
"metadata, chapter names and number, which files in the EPUB differ.\n"
"- **deleted on the e-reader** / **deleted on the computer**: deleting it on "
"the other side is offered, but not checked. Or copy it back.\n"
"- Selecting a row shows what differs or what the state means.\n"
"- **Collections** ([[sammlungen]]) are in the same list, after the books, "
"as \"Collection: name\", with the same states: new, changed on one side, "
"changed on both sides, deleted. For a collection only the books that are on "
"the e-reader count; a book of the collection that is not synced yet is not "
"a difference (KOReader drops entries whose file it cannot find). "
"Placeholders stay on the computer. Before collection.lua on the e-reader is "
"changed, it is backed up.\n"
"\n"
"**Sync n books** (also in the toolbar) asks first, then copies. Always to "
"the same path, so KOReader's .sdr folders (reading position, highlights) "
"fit; they are never touched, not even when deleting. A file that is "
"overwritten or deleted goes to the backup folder first "
"([[einst_speichern]]). Books with unsaved changes in the open library are "
"not overwritten. Afterwards the library is read again and compared once "
"more.\n"
"\n"
"**When it runs**\n"
"- When the e-reader is connected: the linked library is opened instead of "
"the books on the e-reader (or asked, [[einst_kobo]]), then compared. The "
"result goes to the status bar; the sidebar shows **two arrows** (books to "
"sync) and **arrows against each other** (changed on both sides) at Library "
"and E-reader.\n"
"- After saving in the library, and with **Compare**.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:722
msgid "Kobo, Kindle, PocketBook"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:722
msgid ""
"\n"
"KOReader keeps its settings in its own installation folder. Where that is "
"depends on the device:\n"
"\n"
"- **Kobo** (and tolino from 2024 on, Kobo hardware): folder **.kobo** in "
"the root, KOReader in **.adds/koreader**, extra fonts in **fonts**. Model, "
"serial number and firmware come from .kobo/version; an unknown model shows "
"the number on the type plate (e.g. N428) instead.\n"
"- **Kindle**: folders **documents** and **system** in the root, KOReader in "
"**koreader**, extra fonts in **fonts**.\n"
"- **PocketBook**: folder **applications** (with **koreader** inside) and "
"**system/config**, KOReader in **applications/koreader**, the device's own "
"fonts in **system/fonts**.\n"
"\n"
"Kindle and PocketBook put no readable model data on the drive; the area "
"then only shows the kind of device. For the preview choose the model by "
"hand if needed ([[vorschau_bildschirm]]).\n"
"\n"
"Newer Kindles and Android devices only connect via MTP and do not appear as "
"a drive, so they are not found. On Linux a file manager can still copy "
"their files to a folder on the computer; open that folder or use it as the "
"dictionary source. On Windows there is no way from the program.\n"
"\n"
"Only the Kobo was tested on a real device so far; Kindle and PocketBook "
"follow the KOReader source code.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:735
msgid ""
"\n"
"- **Back up** packs the parts ticked on the right into a ZIP file in the "
"subfolder **KOReader** of the backup folder (KOReader_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS.zip). "
"The newest backups are kept ([[einst_speichern]]).\n"
"- **Restore** (or double-click) writes the ticked parts from the selected "
"backup back. Files with the same name are overwritten, others stay. The "
"current state is backed up first (reason \"before restoring\").\n"
"- When a backup is selected, the status bar shows its contents, device and "
"KOReader version; backups from another device are pointed out.\n"
"\n"
"**Parts**\n"
"- **Settings**: settings.reader.lua, defaults.custom.lua.\n"
"- **Statistics and plugin data**: folder settings - reading statistics, "
"history, Bookends presets (settings/bookends_presets), Silverfish.\n"
"- **Book data**: the .sdr folders next to the books - reading position, "
"highlights, notes.\n"
"- **Style tweaks and patches**.\n"
"- **Kobo settings**: Kobo eReader.conf of the Kobo software (off by "
"default, only on a Kobo).\n"
"\n"
"A backup notes the kind of device. It can be restored to another kind (e.g. "
"from the Kobo to a Kindle): the KOReader folder then lands where KOReader "
"is on the connected device. Folders stored in the settings (home folder, "
"last book) still point to the old device; KOReader asks for them again if "
"needed.\n"
"\n"
"After restoring, KOReader takes over the files at the next start, i.e. "
"after ejecting. KOReader writes its settings when it closes; changing "
"something on the e-reader without closing KOReader overwrites the restored "
"state.\n"
"\n"
"The preview can take its profile from a backup too ([[vorschau_profil]]).\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:753 settings.py:794
msgid "Copying dictionaries"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:753
msgid ""
"\n"
"- **Choose source**: a folder on the computer somewhere inside which the "
"dictionaries are ([[einst_kobo]]). It is searched up to 8 levels deep (at "
"most 5000 folders; hidden folders like .adds are searched too, so a copy of "
"the e-reader works as well; only large folders without dictionaries like "
".git, .cache, AppData, Windows and KOReader's plugins, fonts, cache are "
"skipped). A dictionary is a folder with an .ifo file, e.g. My-Dictionary. "
"If there are several folders with the same name, the one with the newest "
".ifo wins; the column **Folder in the source** shows which one was found.\n"
"- Status of each dictionary: **up to date**, **outdated on the e-reader** "
"or **missing on the e-reader** (both red), **only on the e-reader**.\n"
"- **Dictionary** (toolbar) copies the selected ones, without a selection "
"all that are not up to date, to data/dict/<name>/ in the KOReader folder "
"(Kobo: .adds/koreader/data/dict). Files in the target that no longer exist "
"in the source are deleted there.\n"
"- **Double-click** opens the dictionary in the Dictionary area: the "
"registered one that was built into this folder, otherwise the folder from "
"the source itself. A dictionary that is only on the e-reader is opened "
"there after asking; changes are then saved on the e-reader.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:759 settings.py:799
msgid "Ejecting"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:759
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Eject** unmounts the e-reader (Linux: udisksctl, Windows: like \"Eject\" "
"in Explorer). Then the cable can come off.\n"
"\n"
"If something was written to the e-reader in this session (backup restored, "
"dictionary copied, books saved), the program reminds you when quitting. The "
"reminder can be switched off: [[einst_kobo]].\n"
"\n"
"**Library on the e-reader**\n"
"If the opened library is on the e-reader and has unsaved changes, Bookbench "
"asks before ejecting: save, discard or cancel. After ejecting, the library "
"is closed.\n"
"\n"
"If the e-reader disappears without ejecting (cable pulled), a bar above the "
"areas says so. The library stays visible, but saving is refused until the "
"e-reader is back; the changes stay in memory as long as Bookbench is open. "
"**Close library** in the bar (or in the File menu) or **Reload** discards "
"them.\n"
"\n"
"**If ejecting fails**\n"
"On Linux ejecting fails while a program still has a file or folder on the "
"e-reader open, often the file manager that opened when the e-reader was "
"connected. Bookbench first tries three times (thumbnails or the file search "
"often let go by themselves), then lists these programs and offers "
"**Retry**. Only your own programs can be seen.\n"
"- Dolphin: change to another folder, also in other tabs and in the terminal "
"panel, or close the window.\n"
"- Dolphin's terminal panel (F4) follows the folder shown in Dolphin and "
"keeps running when it is hidden: Bookbench then names the terminal panel in "
"a Dolphin window. Opening another folder in Dolphin moves it away.\n"
"- That KDE opens the file manager when the e-reader is connected can be "
"switched off in the KDE system settings (removable storage / device "
"actions).\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:775
msgid ""
"\n"
"The **KOReader workbench**: the desktop KOReader runs invisibly in the "
"background with the settings from your e-reader and shows the page or the "
"start screen as it looks on the device - font, margins, headers and footers "
"from Bookends, the shelves of Bookshelf, style tweaks and patches. Only on "
"Linux and macOS; there is no official KOReader for Windows, so the area is "
"missing there.\n"
"\n"
"**Installing KOReader** (optional, only needed for this)\n"
"- Arch/EndeavourOS: **yay -S koreader-bin**\n"
"- Flatpak: **flatpak install flathub rocks.koreader.KOReader**\n"
"- or put the AppImage from github.com/koreader/koreader/releases into "
"~/Applications or ~/Downloads\n"
"It is searched in this order: own path ([[einst_vorschau]]), koreader in "
"PATH, macOS applications folder, AppImage, Flatpak. Preview menu > **Find "
"KOReader** after installing.\n"
"\n"
"**More**\n"
"- [[vorschau_werkbank]]\n"
"- [[vorschau_lesen]]\n"
"- [[vorschau_patches]]\n"
"- [[vorschau_bookends]]\n"
"- [[vorschau_bookshelf]]\n"
"- [[vorschau_profil]]\n"
"- [[vorschau_rueckweg]]\n"
"- [[vorschau_starten]]\n"
"- [[vorschau_bildschirm]]\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:795
msgid "Workbench"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:795
msgid ""
"\n"
"**View**\n"
"- **Reading page**: the chosen book, opened. ◀ and ▶ turn pages, so does a "
"click into the picture (left third back, otherwise forward, as on the "
"e-reader) and the arrow keys once the picture has the focus.\n"
"- **Start screen**: KOReader without a book - the file browser or "
"Bookshelf, whatever the profile starts with. **With sample books** shows "
"eight sample books with covers; **with the opened library** shows the books "
"of the library opened in Bookbench, with their reading progress (from the "
"book data next to the books) and the most recently read ones first - "
"Bookshelf's top area then shows your current book. The book selected under "
"**Book** counts as the one read last. Nothing is written into the library. "
"The same choice is on the Bookshelf page.\n"
"- Covers: KOReader reads them from the books in the background, the first "
"time for every book. Bookbench keeps KOReader's cover cache between "
"restarts (vorschau/werkbank-cache) and takes new pictures after 1.5, 4, 8 "
"and 15 seconds; **New picture** takes one at any time.\n"
"\n"
"**Tapping in KOReader**\n"
"- On the start screen a click into the picture is a tap at that spot - "
"Bookshelf's shelf buttons (Home, Recent, Series …), its menus, a book. A "
"right-click is a long press.\n"
"- On the reading page a click turns pages; Ctrl+click taps (for example to "
"open KOReader's menus), right-click is a long press.\n"
"- **Swiping**: drag with the left mouse button held down; KOReader gets a "
"swipe in that direction. The **mouse wheel** turns pages on the reading "
"page; on the start screen it swipes up or down (with Shift sideways), for "
"example to scroll Bookshelf's lists.\n"
"- If KOReader does not get to the book within 5 seconds (a question, an "
"unsupported file …), Bookbench says what is on top and shows KOReader's "
"screen; a click taps there, so a dialog can be answered.\n"
"- Books named .kepub (without .epub) are opened as .kepub.epub; KOReader "
"does not know the shorter ending.\n"
"\n"
"**Book**\n"
"- **Sample text** (default): three chapters of Lorem ipsum with title, "
"author and series, enough for the Bookends placeholders.\n"
"- A book of the opened library, or **Other file …** (button below the book "
"selection). Its book data (.sdr) is searched next to the book, on the "
"connected e-reader and in the backup, so font size and margins are those of "
"the e-reader. From the library: right-click on a book > **Open in "
"Preview**.\n"
"\n"
"**How it works**\n"
"KOReader starts without a window (SDL offscreen) at the full resolution of "
"the device ([[vorschau_bildschirm]]); the picture is fitted into the area. "
"Starting takes about a second, every further picture a tenth of a second. "
"Changing the view or the book restarts it; **Restart** does it by hand. "
"Bookbench stops it when closing; if Bookbench crashes, it stops by itself "
"after 30 seconds.\n"
"\n"
"It works on a throw-away copy of the profile (folder vorschau/werkbank), "
"new at every start: reading position, history and settings of the profile "
"stay untouched. Only the plugins Bookends and Bookshelf are loaded - other "
"plugins can lay a full screen over everything. The built-in plugins of "
"KOReader are there as always.\n"
"\n"
"**Open in KOReader** (toolbar) starts the full KOReader in a window as "
"before, for trying out menus: [[vorschau_starten]].\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:819 workbench_reader.py:60
msgid "Font and margins"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:819
msgid ""
"\n"
"Below Preview, **Font and margins** holds KOReader's own general reading "
"settings - what \"Set as default\" in KOReader's reading menu (the bottom "
"menu while reading) stores in settings.reader.lua. The page appears as soon "
"as there is a profile ([[vorschau_profil]]).\n"
"\n"
"**Font**: the font (the family names of the fonts in the profile and in the "
"desktop KOReader; you can also type a name), size, weight, line spacing, "
"word spacing (scaling and reduction), word expansion, kerning, hinting, "
"contrast (gamma), and whether the book's embedded fonts and style are "
"used.\n"
"**Margins**: left and right, top, bottom, and whether top and bottom stay "
"the same. In KOReader's units. The margins of Bookends are on the Bookends "
"page ([[vorschau_bookends]]).\n"
"\n"
"Empty fields use KOReader's defaults. Changes are a draft until **Save to "
"profile**; KOReader reads these values when it opens a book, so the "
"workbench restarts after a change (about a second) and switches to the "
"reading page.\n"
"\n"
"**Values of the book shown**\n"
"KOReader stores font and margins in the book data (.sdr) of every book it "
"has opened once - all of them, when the book is closed. There they win over "
"the general values, which therefore only apply to books never opened "
"before. The section shows which values the book in the workbench brings "
"along.\n"
"**Ignore the book's own values in the workbench** removes them from the "
"copy of the book data in the workbench, so the general values show. The "
"book data of the book itself stays untouched. The sample text never has "
"book data.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:831 workbench_sleep.py:32
msgid "Sleep screen"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:831
msgid ""
"\n"
"Below Preview, **Sleep screen** holds KOReader's sleep screen options "
"(Screen > Sleep screen in KOReader's menu, stored in settings.reader.lua). "
"The page appears as soon as there is a profile ([[vorschau_profil]]).\n"
"\n"
"While the page is open, the workbench shows the sleep screen as the "
"e-reader would when it goes to sleep, without putting KOReader to sleep. "
"**Show again** brings it back (a click into the picture can close it, like "
"a tap on the e-reader); **Back to the page** closes it. Leaving the page "
"closes it as well. KOReader reads these values when it starts, so the "
"workbench restarts after a change (about a second).\n"
"\n"
"**Wallpaper**: cover of the book, a chosen image or book cover, a random "
"image from a folder, reading progress, book status or leave the screen as "
"it is - and for images: border fill, stretch (optionally up to a limit), "
"crop, rotate for best fit, images in order instead of random.\n"
"Paths are the ones on the e-reader (on a Kobo /mnt/onboard/…). **Choose …** "
"takes a file or folder on the connected e-reader. Anything from elsewhere "
"is copied into the profile (folder koreader/screensaver; for a folder only "
"the images directly in it) and goes to the e-reader with **To the e-reader "
"…** ([[vorschau_rueckweg]]). Images elsewhere on the e-reader (outside the "
"KOReader folder) only show in the workbench while it is connected.\n"
"\n"
"**Ignore the book cover**: no cover for books on hold, for finished books, "
"or when the e-reader is locked in the file browser (only with the wallpaper "
"\"cover of the book\").\n"
"\n"
"**Message**: show a message, its text (empty = KOReader's \"Sleeping\"), "
"the shape (box or banner across the screen), its height (0 bottom, 50 "
"middle, 100 top), opacity, and the background fill when the screen is left "
"as it is. **Insert** adds placeholders such as %T title, %A author, %S "
"series, %p percent read, %C chapter title, %P chapter percent, %b battery. "
"The placeholders and the shape/height/opacity exist since KOReader "
"2025.10.\n"
"\n"
"**After waking up**: whether the sleep screen stays after waking (seconds, "
"until a tap, until the \"Exit sleep screen\" gesture), the message for the "
"gesture, and whether reboot/power-off messages are hidden.\n"
"\n"
"**Picture next to the message** (with a picture and a message): KOReader "
"lays the message over the picture. Bookbench's patch "
"2-bookbench-screensaver moves the picture into the free space next to the "
"message instead, so nothing of the cover is hidden ([[vorschau_patches]]). "
"**Add patch** copies it into the profile; with **To the e-reader …** it "
"goes onto the e-reader like any patch. **Keep the picture clear of the "
"message** switches it on and off (on by default once the patch is there); "
"**Keep the picture in the middle** keeps the same gap above and below "
"(smaller picture) instead of using the larger free space. If there is too "
"little room (message in the middle with \"middle\"), KOReader draws as "
"usual. Without the patch in the profile the two switches are hidden.\n"
"\n"
"Fields that do nothing with the current choice are hidden (KOReader's menu "
"greys them out); **Ignore the book cover** only appears with the book cover "
"as wallpaper. Changes are a draft until **Save to profile**.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:849 transfer.py:42 workbench_reader.py:292 workbench_reader.py:308
msgid "Patches"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:849
msgid ""
"\n"
"Below Preview, **Patches** lists the user patches of the profile (folder "
"koreader/patches) as KOReader's patch management does (Tools > More tools > "
"Patch management on the e-reader).\n"
"\n"
"- Patches are grouped by when they run: early once after an update (0), "
"early (1), late after the start (2), before quitting (8), when quitting (9) "
"- the first digit of the file name. Files without such a number or without "
".lua never run and are not listed.\n"
"- ☑ on, ☐ off. Double-click, Space or **On/off** switches. Like KOReader, "
"Bookbench renames the file: off = \".disabled\" behind \".lua\".\n"
"- **Switch off all patches** is KOReader's main switch (file "
".patches_disabled). In the workbench Bookbench switches the patches off one "
"by one instead, so its own patch keeps running.\n"
"- ⚠ marks a patch that failed in the workbench; select it to see KOReader's "
"message. Many patches need the e-reader (a Kobo-only function, the "
"frontlight …) and can never run on the computer - that says nothing about "
"the e-reader.\n"
"- **Show …** opens the patch read-only. **Add …** copies a .lua file into "
"the profile (without a number it gets \"2-\", otherwise KOReader would "
"never run it); **Delete** removes one. Adding and deleting change the "
"profile at once.\n"
"\n"
"**Patches from Bookbench**\n"
"Patches Bookbench brings along, with their state (not in the profile, in "
"the profile, another version) and **Add** or **Update**. They are also "
"attached to every Bookbench release on GitHub, for copying into "
"koreader/patches on the e-reader by hand.\n"
"- **2-bookbench-subtitle**: reads the subtitle and calibre's custom columns "
"straight from each EPUB on the e-reader. Bookshelf gets the token "
"**%subtitle** (top panel, list lines, cover labels, [if:subtitle]); "
"**%calibre{name}** in Bookshelf and Bookends also finds columns stored in "
"the book, without calibre's metadata.calibre (a value in the book wins). "
"Subtitle = the column #subtitle, otherwise the EPUB 3 subtitle. Bookbench "
"writes these columns into the book when you edit them in Metadata. The "
"patch hooks into Bookshelf's and Bookends' insides; if a new version "
"changes them, the values stay empty and nothing else breaks.\n"
"- **2-bookbench-collections** (until 2.4-beta3 called 2-bookbench-stories, "
"which it replaces): collections with series, number, blurb and cover - a "
"series is one entry in KOReader's list of collections, like a folder "
"([[sammlungen_metadaten]]) - and stories inside anthologies as entries of a "
"collection, opened right inside the anthology with a reading position of "
"their own ([[sammlungen_geschichten]]).\n"
"- **2-bookbench-screensaver**: sleep screen with a picture and a message - "
"the picture moves into the free space next to the message instead of lying "
"under it. Switch and options on the page Sleep screen "
"([[vorschau_sperrbildschirm]]). It only adjusts how KOReader puts picture "
"and message together; if KOReader changes that, the sleep screen looks as "
"without the patch.\n"
"\n"
"Switching on and off is a draft until **Save to profile**. Patches run when "
"KOReader starts, so the workbench restarts after every change. To bring "
"patches onto the e-reader: **To the e-reader …** ([[vorschau_rueckweg]]). "
"Bookbench's internal patches (1-bookbench-desktop, 2-bookbench-workbench) "
"are not listed.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:866
msgid "Bookends settings"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:866
msgid ""
"\n"
"If the profile contains the plugin **Bookends**, the sidebar shows "
"**Bookends** below Preview. Its settings page sits in the left column; the "
"picture stays on the right and shows every change after a short pause (a "
"tenth of a second, KOReader keeps running). The workbench switches to the "
"reading page for this.\n"
"\n"
"At the top: the preset shown and **Bookends switched on**. Below them the "
"page has three tabs: **Lines**, **Progress bars** and **Preset** (managing "
"presets, colours, general values). A tab with unsaved changes has a red ●. "
"Within a tab, a click on a bold heading folds a section away or opens it. "
"Bookbench remembers the tab and which sections are open.\n"
"\n"
"**Draft and profile**\n"
"- Changes are a draft at first: they only show in the picture. The red line "
"says so.\n"
"- **Save to profile** (toolbar) writes the preset and the switch into the "
"preview profile; **Discard** goes back to what is in the profile.\n"
"- **To the e-reader …** (toolbar) writes the saved changes to the e-reader: "
"[[vorschau_rueckweg]].\n"
"- **From the e-reader** and **From backup** replace the profile; if "
"Bookbench has changed something in it, it asks first.\n"
"\n"
"**Presets** (tab Preset)\n"
"- Bookends keeps everything you see in presets (one file each). The "
"**active** preset is the one Bookends uses on the e-reader; another one can "
"be shown and edited, the picture then shows that one. **Make active** marks "
"it, saving makes it active in the profile.\n"
"- **New**, **Duplicate**, **Delete**. Name and description are what "
"Bookends shows in its preset manager.\n"
"\n"
"**Lines**\n"
"- Six positions (top and bottom, each left, centre, right), shown as a "
"small page that grows with the column: a click on one shows its lines below "
"(the button shows the first line, +n for more lines, – for an empty "
"position). One line per row. Tokens such as %title, %chap_title or "
"%page_num are filled in by Bookends; **Insert token** lists those of the "
"installed Bookends version, sorted like in Bookends.\n"
"- Below the text field: **Insert token**, **Special characters**, then "
"**B**, **I**, **U** and **Colour …**, which put tags around the selected "
"text of the line you used last (without a selection an empty pair at the "
"cursor): [b]bold[/b], [i]italic[/i], [u]underlined[/u], [c=#RRGGBB]…[/c] "
"for a colour of your choice; **Condition …** and **Examples** see below. "
"That is how a part of a line gets its own colour; the colour of all lines "
"is set on the tab Preset.\n"
"- **Special characters** is a menu with Bookends' symbols (battery, book, "
"arrows, blocks, separators …), in groups as in Bookends. A click inserts at "
"the cursor of the line you used last; \"dynamic\" symbols insert their "
"token (%batt_icon changes with the battery). The symbols come from "
"KOReader's symbol font (nerdfonts/symbols.ttf); Bookbench shows them in the "
"lines as small pictures and takes the font from the profile (fonts of the "
"e-reader) or from the desktop KOReader.\n"
"- **Extra top/bottom margin** (and **extra left/right margin** for the "
"corners) moves only this position away from the edge, in pixels - not the "
"whole header or footer. Bookends calls it the same.\n"
"- Click into a line (section **Selected line**) to set its **style** (bold, "
"italic), **size** (empty = the general size), **font**, **capitals** and "
"**move line**: ← → and ↑ ↓ in pixels, negative = left or up, **Back to 0** "
"sets both to 0. Inserting or deleting lines keeps the settings with their "
"line.\n"
"- **Font**: \"Default\" is Bookends' general font. Then the font families "
"(UI font, serif, sans-serif …; in brackets the font KOReader takes for "
"them, set in KOReader under the font menu), then one entry per font family "
"of the profile - the fonts of the e-reader. Bookbench stores the path on "
"the e-reader, as Bookends does; in the workbench it uses the copy in the "
"profile. A font from another e-reader that is not in the profile shows with "
"⚠.\n"
"- **Show on**: all pages, or only odd or even pages (for example page "
"numbers on the outer side).\n"
"- **Bar in this line**: the token %bar draws a progress bar inside the "
"line. **Insert bar** adds it, **Remove bar** takes it out again. Only for a "
"line with a bar the bar fields appear: chapter or book, chapter ticks (book "
"only), style, fill direction, thickness and unread thickness in pixels "
"(empty thickness = as high as the text), then colours and markers as for "
"the progress bars (below).\n"
"- **Conditions in this line**: Bookends shows a part of a line only under a "
"condition, written into the text as [if:condition]then[else]otherwise[/if] "
"- for example [if:batt<20]LOW %batt_icon[/if] or "
"[if:page=odd]%title[else]%chap_title[/if]. The list shows the conditions of "
"the selected line (nested ones below their outer one; the one at the cursor "
"is selected).\n"
"- **New …** (or **Condition …** below the text field) opens a window: one "
"or more parts, each with **not**, a field from Bookends' list (battery, "
"Wi-Fi, page odd/even, book %, time, weekday, series …; **Other field …** "
"for anything else, such as calibre{mood}), a comparison (=, ≠, <, >, ≤, ≥ "
"or \"has a value\") and a value; joined by **and** or **or**. Below: the "
"finished condition, the **then** text and optionally an **otherwise** text. "
"Selected text of the line becomes the then text.\n"
"- **Edit …** (or double-click) opens the same window for the selected "
"condition. Conditions with brackets or a mix of and/or are edited as text "
"(**Edit as text**). **Dissolve** removes a condition and keeps its then "
"text.\n"
"- **Examples** (below the text field) inserts Bookends' ready-made lines "
"(Kindle-style time left, battery bars, weekend …).\n"
"\n"
"**Progress bars**\n"
"Up to eight bars across the page, independent of the lines. Choose a bar in "
"the list (✓ = switched on, with its edge), then **Switched on**; only a "
"switched-on bar shows its fields: chapter or book, chapter ticks, style, "
"fill direction, **Edge** (top, bottom, left, right), thickness (diameter "
"for round bars) and unread thickness, distance from the edge and the "
"margins at both ends. A bar set to \"Position relative to line text\" on "
"the e-reader shows its distance from the line text instead; switching that "
"on or off stays on the e-reader.\n"
"- **Own colours and ticks**: switched off, Bookends takes the colours of "
"the style. Switched on: colour of the read and unread part, chapter ticks, "
"border and ticks on the read part (click a field: choose a colour, back to "
"the default, or transparent), whether the tick colour is inverted on the "
"read part, tick height (%), tick width (× the normal width) and border "
"thickness.\n"
"- **Markers**: a small mark on the bar at a reading position - start of the "
"reading session, where the book was opened, bookmarks, or today's start. "
"One on top, one below; for each the style (chevron or solid), size (%), "
"offset (px) and colour.\n"
"\n"
"**Colours** (tab Preset)\n"
"Text colour (default = the colour of the book text), symbol colour (default "
"= like the text) and the background behind the top and bottom lines "
"(default = none) - for all lines of the preset. On a black-and-white screen "
"Bookends shows colours as grey. If the e-reader of the profile has no "
"colour screen (model table, or the device chosen under View), every colour "
"field - here, for bars, markers and the Grey … button below the text field "
"- asks for a grey in % black instead, as Bookends' own menu does there, and "
"colours set elsewhere show as the grey the e-reader will display.\n"
"\n"
"**General** (tab Preset)\n"
"Font size and scale, the four margins, the gap before texts overlap and "
"what is shortened first. Empty fields use Bookends' defaults.\n"
"\n"
"Bookends is a plugin by AndyHazz (github.com/AndyHazz/bookends.koplugin); "
"Bookbench only reads and writes its files. Newer Bookends versions can "
"change the format; unknown parts stay as they are.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:908
msgid "Bookshelf settings"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:908
msgid ""
"\n"
"If the profile contains the plugin **Bookshelf**, the sidebar shows "
"**Bookshelf** below Preview. The workbench switches to the start screen for "
"this.\n"
"\n"
"Bookshelf cannot reload its settings while KOReader runs. After a change, "
"the workbench therefore restarts after a short pause (about a second) and "
"then shows the new start screen.\n"
"\n"
"**Draft and profile** work as for Bookends ([[vorschau_bookends]]): first "
"only in the picture, **Save to profile** keeps them, **Discard** goes "
"back.\n"
"\n"
"The page has five tabs: **Appearance** (theme, wallpaper, plank, "
"decoration, colours), **Home** (top panel and its modules), **Navigation** "
"(start menu, shelves, footer and buttons), **Layout** (columns and rows, "
"font sizes, covers and spines, display) and **All** (all keys in the file). "
"A tab with unsaved changes has a red ●. Within a tab, a click on a bold "
"heading folds a section away or opens it. Bookbench remembers the tab and "
"which sections are open.\n"
"\n"
"**Theme**\n"
"- **Default theme**: the theme every shelf wears unless it has its own. "
"**Custom theme** is your own look (wallpaper, plank, colours below), "
"**Plain** Bookshelf's plain look, and every theme pack in the profile. A "
"theme is a layer over the custom theme: it replaces only the parts it "
"brings.\n"
"- **Light or dark**: automatic (like KOReader's night mode) or fixed.\n"
"- **Theme per shelf**: each shelf can wear its own theme or follow the "
"default.\n"
"- Theme packs are folders in settings/bookshelf/ornaments with a "
"theme/theme.json. New ones come from Bookshelf's theme library on the "
"e-reader; take the profile again afterwards.\n"
"\n"
"**Wallpaper, plank, colours** (of the custom theme)\n"
"- **Wallpaper**: a picture from settings/bookshelf/wallpapers, a second one "
"for full screen shelves, how strongly the panels over it are shaded, "
"transparent buttons, blur under the panels, inverting in night mode. **Add "
"picture …** copies a picture into the profile at once.\n"
"- **Plank**: oak, colour only, or a plank from a theme pack.\n"
"- **Colours**: day and night colour for each part (text, progress bar, "
"plank, menu bar …). Click a field to choose; \"-\" is the default, ✕ resets "
"day and night. Grey is stored as Bookshelf does (as a grey level). Without "
"a colour screen on the profile's e-reader a click asks for a grey in % "
"black, and colours show as the grey the e-reader will display.\n"
"\n"
"**Decoration (ornaments)**\n"
"- **How often per shelf**: none, rarely, often (default), always. Ornaments "
"only appear on spine shelves.\n"
"- The list shows all ornaments of the profile (loose files and packs); a "
"double-click switches a piece or a whole pack off and on.\n"
"\n"
"**Top panel**\n"
"- **Shows**: the current book (hero card) or Bookshelf's modules (clock, "
"quote …). Arranging the modules stays in Bookshelf.\n"
"- **Lines of the hero card**: status line, rating, title, author, metadata, "
"description, tags, progress (✓ = shown). For each: **Shown**, the text with "
"tokens (**Insert token** lists Bookshelf's tokens), size, alignment, bold, "
"capitals; **Default** goes back to Bookshelf's default for that line. "
"[if:token]…[/if] shows a part only if the token has a value; an empty text "
"leaves the line out.\n"
"- Rating and tags are buttons, not text: only on/off, size and alignment; "
"for tags also which kinds (authors, series, collections, genres, folder) "
"and how many rows.\n"
"- **Subtitle** puts the book's subtitle into the line "
"([if:subtitle]%subtitle[/if]; in the metadata line instead of the series). "
"It needs the patch 2-bookbench-subtitle in the profile and on the e-reader "
"([[vorschau_patches]]).\n"
"\n"
"**Modules in the top panel**\n"
"- The modules shown when the top panel is set to modules, with their "
"position: page, column and row of the top-left cell, width and height in "
"grid cells - or **Place automatically**. The panel has 1 or 2 rows and 2 to "
"8 columns depending on the screen; Bookshelf moves a module that does not "
"fit or overlaps another.\n"
"- **Add** offers Bookshelf's modules (clock, quote, weather …). Three ask "
"for a choice in a submenu: **Action** (one of Bookshelf's standard actions: "
"reading calendar, Wi-Fi, night mode, sleep …), **Shelf** (a shelf with "
"books, not a stack) and **Ornament** (a piece from the profile). **Choice** "
"changes it for the selected module; the list shows it after the module's "
"name. **Up**/**Down** change the order, **Remove** takes one out.\n"
"\n"
"**Start menu**\n"
"- The entries of Bookshelf's start menu as a tree: entries, modules (▣), "
"dividers and folders (one level deep, as in Bookshelf).\n"
"- **Add**: a folder, a divider, a module, or one of Bookshelf's standard "
"entries (reading calendar, Wi-Fi, night mode, sleep …; the name is stored "
"in Bookbench's language). It goes behind the selected entry.\n"
"- **Up**/**Down** within its level, **Folder** moves an entry into a folder "
"or out of it, **Rename …** (or double-click), **Remove**. Other actions and "
"the icons are set in Bookshelf's start menu editor.\n"
"\n"
"**Shelves**\n"
"- The shelves above the books: ☑ shown, ☐ hidden (double-click or "
"**On/off**; one stays shown), order, name, and how a shelf is shown "
"(covers, list, spines, automatic).\n"
"- **Add > New shelf …** makes a shelf: the source (whole library, recently "
"read, latest added, favourites, your own collection, a folder of the "
"e-reader - as folders or flattened -, a stack for each series, author, "
"genre … or a shelf of shelves), the name (follows the source until you "
"change it) and how it is shown. Sorting starts as Bookshelf sets it for a "
"new shelf; sorting and filters are changed in Bookshelf. **Add** also "
"brings back a standard shelf that was removed (home, recent, series …).\n"
"- As long as Bookshelf has not written modules, start menu or shelves into "
"its file, it uses its defaults: Bookbench shows these (with a note) and "
"writes them with the first change, with the names in Bookbench's language - "
"as Bookshelf does in its own.\n"
"\n"
"**More settings**\n"
"- A selection of settings that change the look (footer and buttons on the "
"tab Navigation; columns and rows, font sizes, covers and spines, display on "
"the tab Layout), with Bookshelf's defaults where the file has no value.\n"
"- **All** (all keys in the file): everything in "
"settings/bookshelf/settings.lua, also what is not on the other tabs. "
"Double-click (or **Change …**) changes a simple value (number, text, "
"on/off) in its type. Tables - shelves, modules, the start menu - can only "
"be changed in Bookshelf itself or on the other tabs (colours, theme and "
"ornaments per shelf, the hero card, modules, start menu, shelves). "
"**Default** removes the key, then Bookshelf's default applies. Changed keys "
"have a ●.\n"
"- Values Bookshelf writes by itself while running (current shelf and page, "
"version notes) are not listed.\n"
"\n"
"Bookshelf is a plugin by AndyHazz (github.com/AndyHazz/bookshelf.koplugin); "
"Bookbench only reads and writes its settings file.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:959 settings.py:512 settings.py:1082 transfer_window.py:41
msgid "Profile"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:959
msgid ""
"\n"
"The preview runs in its own profile in the settings folder (subfolder "
"vorschau, [[dateien]]). A normal KOReader installation on the computer "
"stays untouched.\n"
"\n"
"- **From the e-reader** rebuilds the profile from the connected e-reader: "
"settings.reader.lua, folder settings (with the Bookends presets), "
"styletweaks, patches, the plugins you installed (the built-in ones come "
"with the desktop KOReader) and the fonts from KOReader's fonts folder and "
"from the font folder of the device ([[kobo_geraete]]).\n"
"- **From backup** takes settings, settings folder, styletweaks and patches "
"from the backup selected in the E-reader area, otherwise from the newest "
"([[kobo_sicherungen]]). Plugins and fonts are in no backup; they stay as "
"they are. So you have to take the profile from the e-reader at least once.\n"
"- When taking a profile and before every start, these are removed from the "
"settings: **screen_dpi** (it would override the screen size of the "
"preview), plugin paths and the last files/folders on the e-reader. In the "
"profile, book data is always next to the book.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:966 transfer_window.py:33 transfer_window.py:202 transfer_window.py:211 transfer_window.py:229
msgid "To the e-reader"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:966
msgid ""
"\n"
"**To the e-reader …** (toolbar) writes what you changed in the preview "
"profile to the connected e-reader - the way back after Bookends, Bookshelf, "
"font and margins, patches.\n"
"\n"
"**What is offered**\n"
"- When the profile is taken from the e-reader (or from a backup), Bookbench "
"notes the state of the files it compares (folder vorschau/basis). Offered "
"is what changed in the profile since then: Bookends presets and "
"bookends.lua, Bookshelf's settings, wallpapers and ornaments, patches (also "
"switched off = renamed, added, deleted), style tweaks, and KOReader's "
"settings (settings.reader.lua) key by key.\n"
"- ⚠ **Conflict**: the same file or key was changed on the e-reader too "
"since the profile was taken. Not preselected - transferring it replaces the "
"e-reader's change.\n"
"- What changed only on the e-reader (KOReader writes all the time: "
"positions, statistics) is left alone. Databases (statistics, cover cache) "
"are never transferred.\n"
"- Device-specific settings are never offered: screen DPI, paths and last "
"files, frontlight, Wi-Fi, migration notes and the like. **Never offer** "
"hides further settings for good (it only applies to keys, not files).\n"
"- A profile taken before 1.4-beta6 has no noted state: then profile and "
"e-reader are compared directly, and only areas Bookbench has changed are "
"preselected. Take the profile again to get the exact comparison.\n"
"\n"
"**Transferring**\n"
"- Tick what should go (click the box, Space, **On/off**, **All**, **None**, "
"**Suggestion**) and **Transfer**. Before writing, the KOReader settings of "
"the e-reader are backed up (E-reader area, reason \"before writing from the "
"preview\").\n"
"- Settings are written key by key into the e-reader's settings.reader.lua; "
"everything else in it stays.\n"
"- Unsaved drafts on the workbench pages are asked about first.\n"
"- Afterwards eject the e-reader; KOReader reads the changes at its next "
"start. If KOReader is running on the e-reader while you transfer (rare over "
"USB), it may overwrite settings.reader.lua when it closes.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:982
msgid "Starting and output"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:982
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Open in KOReader** (toolbar, always the first button)\n"
"- Starts the full desktop KOReader in a window, with menus and all plugins "
"of the profile - for trying things out. The workbench keeps running beside "
"it ([[vorschau_werkbank]]).\n"
"- It shows what the workbench shows: the reading page with the selected "
"book, or the start screen (with sample books or the opened library, the "
"selected book as the one read last).\n"
"- Since 1.4-beta6 it works on its own throw-away copy of the profile "
"(vorschau/fenster), with the drafts of the workbench pages.\n"
"- **Changes from KOReader** (since 2.2-beta5): when the window is closed, "
"Bookbench shows what you changed in KOReader's menus - KOReader settings "
"key by key, Bookends presets and switches, Bookshelf, patches, style "
"tweaks, sleep screen images - and you choose what goes into the profile "
"(like **To the e-reader …**, with **Never offer** for keys you never want). "
"Values KOReader and the plugins write by themselves (positions, caches, "
"window size, Bookshelf's own state) are left out. ⚠ = changed in the "
"profile too since the window was opened - not preselected. Later: Preview > "
"**Changes from the KOReader window …**, until KOReader is opened again. "
"From the profile, **To the e-reader …** brings the changes to the "
"e-reader.\n"
"- The selected book (the sample text too) is copied with its book data "
"(.sdr) into that copy and opened there. KOReader writes to the book data "
"while reading; that way the original stays untouched, on the e-reader too.\n"
"- Book data is searched: next to the book, on the connected e-reader (same "
"file name), in the backup. Without book data the general settings apply, so "
"font size and margins may differ.\n"
"- **Other file …** (below the book selection) shows a book that is not in "
"the opened library.\n"
"\n"
"**KOReader output**\n"
"Under **Profile, device and output** (click to unfold) is what KOReader "
"reports at start and while running: the workbench's output "
"(vorschau/werkbank/koreader.log), after **Open in KOReader** that of the "
"window (vorschau/fenster/koreader.log). After it closes, the status bar "
"gives the number of warnings and errors, for example a patch that does not "
"run on the computer because it needs something specific to the e-reader.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:995
msgid "Screen and device"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:995
msgid ""
"\n"
"The workbench and the window copy the screen of a device: size in pixels, "
"pixel density, colour or black and white (black and white switches off the "
"colours in KOReader, as on the device). The workbench always uses the full "
"size. Which device it is stands under **Profile, device and output**:\n"
"- **Automatic** (default): the device the profile was taken from (From the "
"e-reader, or the device noted in the backup), otherwise the connected "
"e-reader, otherwise the Libra Colour - then with a ⚠.\n"
"- Kindle and PocketBook note no model, so automatic falls back to the Libra "
"Colour for them; choose **Own size** there.\n"
"- **Settings > Device > Preview**: a fixed Kobo or tolino model, or **Own "
"size** for other e-readers with KOReader (pixels in portrait format and ppi "
"from the data sheet) - [[einst_vorschau]].\n"
"\n"
"For **Open in KOReader** (window) the size is chosen there: a Libra Colour "
"has 1264 × 1680 pixels at 300 dpi; hardly any screen is that tall.\n"
"- **Fitted to the screen** takes the height of the screen and lowers the "
"DPI to match; KOReader sizes fonts and spacing by the DPI, so the page "
"looks the same, only coarser.\n"
"- **Half size**: half the pixels and half the DPI.\n"
"- **Full size** is pixel-exact, but the window then goes beyond the "
"screen.\n"
"A screenshot from KOReader (menu or gesture) always has the full resolution "
"of the window.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1007 help_topics.py:1083 settings.py:50 settings.py:344
msgid "Saving and backups"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1007
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Save** (Ctrl+S) writes all applied changes (yellow) of all books: chapter "
"names and levels, blurbs, series, covers. Changes that were not applied "
"(red) are not saved.\n"
"\n"
"**Backups**\n"
"- Before saving, every affected file is copied to the backup folder: "
"**<name>_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS.epub**. The newest backups of each book are kept "
"([[einst_speichern]]).\n"
"- Do not put the backup folder on the e-reader: otherwise KOReader shows "
"the backups as books. The default is a folder in the user directory.\n"
"- To go back: copy the backup over the book (rename it back to the original "
"name).\n"
"\n"
"**What is written**\n"
"- Only the files inside the EPUB that changed (nav.xhtml, toc.ncx, "
"content.opf, the cover image); everything else is copied unchanged. The "
"file \"mimetype\" stays first and uncompressed, as the EPUB rules require.\n"
"- Saving goes to a temporary file first and replaces the book only when "
"that worked.\n"
"\n"
"After saving, KOReader only shows new metadata after re-reading the book "
"information (Cover browser > Extract and cache book information).\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1021
msgid ""
"\n"
"File > **Settings …** opens the settings. The tree on the left has groups "
"with pages below them; a group page shows what its pages are for. **Help** "
"(or F1) in the dialog opens the chapter of the page shown here.\n"
"\n"
"**OK** saves, **Cancel** forgets the changes. Most settings apply at once, "
"the language too (without a restart); chapter settings after reloading "
"(F5).\n"
"\n"
"All settings are in **einstellungen.json** in the settings folder "
"([[dateien]]). Export, import and resetting: [[einst_erweitert]].\n"
"\n"
"**Pages**\n"
"- General: [[einst_darstellung]], [[einst_oberflaeche]], [[einst_start]]\n"
"- [[einst_speichern]]\n"
"- Books: [[einst_bibliothek]], [[einst_cleaner]], [[einst_kapitel]], "
"[[einst_klappentexte]] ([[einst_klappentexte_profile]]), [[einst_cover]] "
"([[einst_cover_nachschlagen]]), [[einst_metadaten]]\n"
"- Device: [[einst_kobo]], [[einst_vorschau]]\n"
"- [[einst_online]]\n"
"- [[einst_erweitert]]\n"
"\n"
"Options that only matter with another setting (e.g. the tooltip delay, the "
"Claude model) only appear when that setting is on.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1038 settings.py:45 settings.py:204
msgid "Language and theme"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1038
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Language** (default: automatic)\n"
"- Automatic: German if the system language is German, otherwise English "
"(Windows: language of the interface; Linux: LANGUAGE, LC_ALL, LC_MESSAGES, "
"LANG).\n"
"- The change applies at once, without a restart: folder, area, selected "
"book, unsaved changes and window stay.\n"
"\n"
"**Theme** (default: like the system)\n"
"- Light and dark use the colours of the Keller design guide (Qt style "
"Fusion).\n"
"- Like the system: style and colours of the desktop, checked at start. On "
"KDE with the system's PySide6 (Arch: pyside6) that is Breeze, as in the "
"other KDE programs; with PySide6 from pip it is Fusion with the colours of "
"the system.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1047 settings.py:46 settings.py:223
msgid "Interface"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1047
msgid ""
"\n"
"- **Status strip next to the lists** (default: on): coloured dots and "
"pictograms ([[status]]). Also under View.\n"
"- **Show tooltips** (default: on) and **Delay** (default: 600 ms): "
"explanations when the mouse stays over a button.\n"
"- **Show cover in Table of contents** (default: on) and **Height** "
"(default: 96 px): the small cover next to the book title; a click shows it "
"large. Also under View > Cover.\n"
"- **Double-click on a book opens it in** (default: Table of contents): "
"Table of contents, Metadata, the Preview (the book is selected there) or "
"KOReader (selects it in the Preview and starts the desktop KOReader). "
"Preview and KOReader only where the Preview exists (not on Windows). A "
"right-click on a book in the library offers all of them.\n"
"- **Sidebar expanded** (default: on): with names and counters, or only "
"symbols. Ctrl+B switches at any time; the last state is remembered.\n"
"\n"
"Which pictograms are shown where: [[einst_marken]].\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1056 settings.py:47 settings.py:249
msgid "Status symbols"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1056
msgid ""
"\n"
"One row per pictogram ([[status]]), one column per place:\n"
"- **Sidebar**: the pictograms with numbers next to the areas.\n"
"- **Book lists**: the narrow lists in Table of contents and Metadata, and "
"the list of Book series.\n"
"- **Library**: the column Status in the library.\n"
"\n"
"Default: everything on. A switched-off pictogram is only hidden; the "
"filters (e.g. No cover) and sorting by status still count it. The coloured "
"dot for changed, applied and saved is not affected; the whole strip next to "
"the lists can be switched off under [[einst_oberflaeche]]. **All on** and "
"**All off** set every box.\n"
"\n"
"Dictionary, e-reader and \"connected\" only exist in the sidebar; gaps in a "
"series only in the sidebar and in Book series.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1066 settings.py:48 settings.py:290
msgid "Startup"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1066
msgid ""
"\n"
"- **Open the last folder** (default: on): the books folder that was open "
"when the program was last closed, if it still exists - for the e-reader "
"only when it is connected. Off: the program starts empty; File > Recently "
"opened still has the folders.\n"
"- **Area at start** (default: Library): always the library, or the area "
"that was shown last.\n"
"\n"
"A folder given on the command line (start.py <folder>) is always opened.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1072 settings.py:49 settings.py:300 updates.py:82 updates.py:90 updates.py:270
msgid "Updates"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1072
msgid ""
"\n"
"**This copy** says how Bookbench was installed: with the Windows setup, "
"with install.sh or not at all (source code, e.g. a git clone). Only "
"installed copies can update themselves ([[updates]]). **Check now** looks "
"right away, with the values on this page (also before OK).\n"
"\n"
"**Automatic check** (default: at start, at most once a day): off, at every "
"start, at most once a day or once a week. It runs a few seconds after the "
"start in the background. Only a newer version opens a window; without "
"network there is only a note in the status line. Source code copies never "
"check automatically.\n"
"\n"
"**Skipped version**: shown when a version was skipped in the update window. "
"**Show again** brings its window back at the next check.\n"
"\n"
"**Include pre-releases** (default: off): also releases marked as "
"pre-release on GitHub.\n"
"\n"
"The releases come from github.com/kepfle-git/Bookbench; that is fixed in "
"the program. Without signing in GitHub allows 60 requests per hour, far "
"more than the check needs.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1083
msgid ""
"\n"
"- **Backup folder** (default: Bookbench Backups in the user folder): books "
"are copied here before each save ([[speichern]]). The E-reader area uses "
"its subfolder KOReader, the dictionary its subfolder Wörterbücher. Not on "
"the e-reader.\n"
"- **Backups per book** (default: 5): the newest ones are kept, older ones "
"are deleted when saving.\n"
"- **Backups of the KOReader settings** (default: 10): backups made in the "
"E-reader area; this many stay in the subfolder KOReader, including the "
"backup before each restore ([[kobo_sicherungen]]).\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1088 settings.py:52 settings.py:368
msgid "Library and import"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1088
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Folder scheme**\n"
"- **This library**: only when a library on this computer is open. Where Add "
"books puts new books ([[bibliothek_ordnerschema]]). **Detect from the "
"books** looks for the scheme most books follow; the line below shows an "
"example with a book of the library, or what is wrong with the scheme. Saved "
"in the library folder (.bookbench/library.json).\n"
"- **Default for new libraries** (default: "
"{author_sort}/[{series}/][{series_index:2} - ]{title}, like Calibre): used "
"when a library has no scheme of its own and none can be detected. **Reset "
"to Calibre-like default** puts it back.\n"
"\n"
"**Reading cache**\n"
"- **Remember read books** (default: on): Bookbench remembers what it read "
"from each book (table of contents, metadata, cover size) together with size "
"and date of the file, and only reads changed files again. KOReader's book "
"data (.sdr) counts too. Stored per library in the settings folder "
"(einlese-cache), not in the library folder.\n"
"- **Empty reading cache**: the next opening reads all books again.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1097 settings.py:53 settings.py:439 settings.py:987
msgid "Advert text"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1097
msgid ""
"\n"
"Patterns for Remove advert text ([[bibliothek_cleaner]]).\n"
"\n"
"- One pattern per line, case does not matter (default: OceanofPDF.com).\n"
"- A line in **/slashes/** is a regular expression, e.g. /downloaded from "
"\\S+/ for \"Downloaded from\" followed by any address.\n"
"- The line below shows how many patterns there are, or what is wrong with a "
"regular expression. Invalid patterns are not saved.\n"
"- **Reset to default** puts back OceanofPDF.com.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1105
msgid ""
"\n"
"- **Subtitle lines per chapter** (default: 1): how many lines below a "
"chapter number make up the suggested name. Some books have three (e.g. "
"place, time, person); with 3 all of them are joined with \" / \".\n"
"- **Write entries in capitals normally** (default: off): THE SHIRE becomes "
"The Shire, A LONG-EXPECTED PARTY becomes A Long-Expected Party. Small words "
"(a, of, the …) stay lower case except at the start.\n"
"\n"
"Both apply after reloading (F5), because the suggestions are made when "
"reading the books. See also [[kapitel]].\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1111 settings.py:55 settings.py:476
msgid "Blurbs"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1111
msgid ""
"\n"
"The page shows the order of the sources ([[klappentexte_nachschlagen]]).\n"
"\n"
"- **Ask Google Books and Open Library first** (default: on): free, no key. "
"Off: every look-up goes to Claude straight away.\n"
"- The own Google Books key is under [[einst_online]].\n"
"- **Preferred language when looking up** (default: same as the book): "
"another language looks for the blurb of an edition in that language, "
"without the ISBN, for the free sources and for Claude's source profiles "
"([[klappentexte_nachschlagen]]). It is also the default language in the "
"window Look up metadata ([[metadaten_nachschlagen]]). Blurbs already found "
"or applied stay as they are.\n"
"- **Shortest blurb** (default: 80 characters): shorter texts from a source "
"are not taken - often only a teaser line or \"A novel\".\n"
"- **Longest blurb** (default: 4000 characters): longer texts are not taken "
"- usually a plot summary or a whole article.\n"
"The limits apply to all sources (free ones and Claude), not to text you "
"enter yourself.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1121
msgid ""
"\n"
"The list of profiles in the order in which they are checked. The first "
"active one that fits a book is used; what the fields mean: "
"[[klappentexte_profile]].\n"
"\n"
"- **New …** puts a new profile at the top (otherwise the general web search "
"would come first).\n"
"- **Edit …** (or double-click), **Delete**, **Up**, **Down**.\n"
"- **Defaults** puts the included profiles back; your own profiles are "
"removed.\n"
"- \"(off)\" in front of \"Applies to\": the profile is switched off and "
"skipped.\n"
"\n"
"Changes count after OK. If a profile changes, Claude looks up again for its "
"books; books it already found stay as they are.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1131 settings.py:64 settings.py:524
msgid "Online services"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1131
msgid ""
"\n"
"Keys for the services Bookbench asks on the internet; both are stored in "
"plain text in einstellungen.json ([[dateien]]) - keep that in mind when "
"exporting the settings. What is sent where: [[kosten]].\n"
"\n"
"**Google Books**\n"
"- **Own Google Books API key** (default: empty): for looking up blurbs and "
"covers. Without a key Google Books often answers \"quota exceeded\", "
"because everyone without a key shares one daily quota. An own key is free: "
"in the Google Cloud Console create a project, enable the **Books API** and "
"create an **API key** under Credentials. If Google rejects it, the look-up "
"says so.\n"
"\n"
"**Claude API** (blurbs from source profiles, dictionary entries)\n"
"- **API key**: from console.anthropic.com (account with credit). Empty: the "
"environment variable ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is used. Model and cost estimate "
"only appear with a key.\n"
"- **Model** (default: claude-haiku-5-5): choose from the list; Haiku is "
"enough for looking things up and is the cheapest. **Load list** asks "
"Anthropic with the API key for the models available right now (newest "
"first). A name can also be typed in.\n"
"- **Estimated cost per book** (default: 0.015 $): only for the question "
"before looking up all missing blurbs; the real cost depends on page length "
"and searches.\n"
"\n"
"Also needed: the package **anthropic** (pip install anthropic). What is "
"sent: [[kosten]].\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1144 plugins.py:702 settings.py:58 settings.py:649
msgid "Covers"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1144
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Inserting a new cover** ([[cover_bild]])\n"
"- **Shrink to at most this height** (default: 0 = never): your e-reader "
"shows at most its screen height (e.g. 1680 px on a Libra Colour); larger "
"images only make the file bigger. Images are never enlarged.\n"
"- **JPEG quality** (default: 90): only when an image is converted or "
"shrunk; otherwise it is taken unchanged. 90 is barely distinguishable from "
"the original; below 75 artefacts become visible.\n"
"\n"
"**Marking**\n"
"- **Mark covers below this height** (default: 800 px, 0 = never): ⚠ in the "
"Metadata area and its filter \"Small cover\".\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1152
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Sources** (both free, no key needed; Google Books uses your own key if "
"there is one, [[einst_online]]; [[kosten]])\n"
"- **Open Library** (default: on): often large scans, sometimes of another "
"edition.\n"
"- **Google Books** (default: on): the publisher's image, usually smaller.\n"
"\n"
"**Which images count**\n"
"- **Smallest height** (default: 300 px): smaller images are left out "
"(preview thumbnails, placeholders).\n"
"- **At most this many to choose from** (default: 8): each image is "
"downloaded, so more takes longer. Looking up all missing covers takes at "
"most 3 per book and uses the largest.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1161 settings.py:60 settings.py:677
msgid "Metadata and Calibre"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1161
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Calibre**\n"
"- **Compare with Calibre** (default: on): Bookbench reads Calibre's "
"metadata.calibre (root of the e-reader, or the opened folder and the "
"folders above it) and shows its values: in [[metadaten_calibre]] for "
"comparing, in the library and in Blurbs for taking over series and blurbs "
"([[bibliothek_calibre]]).\n"
"- Off: metadata.calibre is ignored and the Calibre buttons and menu entries "
"disappear. Useful once Bookbench has replaced Calibre for you.\n"
"- Switching off applies at once, switching on after reloading (F5).\n"
"- The one-time import from a Calibre library (metadata.db) works "
"independently of this setting: [[calibre_import]].\n"
"\n"
"**Fields**\n"
"- **Publication date** (default: on): shows the field Published in the form "
"(dc:date in the book) and lets the library show the column Published "
"(hidden at first, View > Library columns).\n"
"\n"
"**Own fields** ([[metadaten_felder]])\n"
"- The list holds one Calibre column per row: **lookup name** (as in "
"Calibre, with #, e.g. #subtitle) and the **label** Bookbench shows. "
"Default: none.\n"
"- Select a row to change it below, **Add / change** takes the two fields "
"(an existing lookup name is changed, a new one added). **Delete**, **Up** "
"and **Down** work on the selected row; the order is the order in the form.\n"
"- **From Calibre** adds all own columns found in the metadata.calibre of "
"the opened folder, with Calibre's labels. Delete the ones you do not want.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1176
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Detection**\n"
"- **Look for the e-reader every** (default: 3 seconds): how often the "
"program checks whether an e-reader is connected or unplugged ([[kobo]]).\n"
"- **When an e-reader is connected** (default: open its books, ask if "
"another folder is open): or always ask, or do nothing ([[kobo]]). If the "
"e-reader is linked with a library on this computer, \"its books\" means "
"that library; it is compared with the e-reader afterwards "
"([[kobo_abgleich]]).\n"
"\n"
"**Copying dictionaries**\n"
"- **Copy dictionaries from (folder on the computer)**: where the "
"dictionaries to copy are ([[kobo_woerterbuecher]]). Also set by Choose "
"source in the E-reader area.\n"
"\n"
"How many KOReader backups are kept: [[einst_speichern]].\n"
"\n"
"**Ejecting**\n"
"- **Remind me to eject when quitting** (default: on): only if something was "
"written to the e-reader in this session ([[kobo_auswerfen]]).\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1189
msgid ""
"\n"
"Only on Linux and macOS.\n"
"\n"
"- **Desktop KOReader** (default: empty = search): only fill in if it is not "
"found by itself ([[vorschau]]). **flatpak** always takes the Flatpak "
"rocks.koreader.KOReader, also when KOReader is installed normally too "
"([[fehler]]).\n"
"- **Device shown** (default: automatic): automatic, any Kobo or tolino "
"model from the list (with size and ppi), or **Own size** with width, height "
"(pixels in portrait format), ppi and colour screen for other e-readers with "
"KOReader. See [[vorschau_bildschirm]].\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1195 settings.py:65 settings.py:859
msgid "Advanced"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1195
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Files of the program**\n"
"- Shows where settings, chapter decisions and blurb cache are "
"([[dateien]]); **Open settings folder** opens it in the file manager.\n"
"\n"
"**Blurb cache**\n"
"- Shows how many entries it has. **Empty …** deletes it: afterwards "
"everything is looked up again, and Claude costs again.\n"
"\n"
"**Export and import**\n"
"- **Export settings …** writes all settings to a JSON file, e.g. to take "
"them to another computer. It contains the API key.\n"
"- **Import settings …** replaces the current settings with those from the "
"file (unknown entries are ignored). The language too applies at once.\n"
"\n"
"**Reset**\n"
"- **Reset all settings …** puts everything back to the defaults. Kept: "
"opened folders, the list of dictionaries, window size, language and the API "
"key.\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1209
msgid "Installing and updating"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1209
msgid ""
"\n"
"**Installing**\n"
"- **Windows**: Bookbench_Setup_<version>.exe from the release page on "
"GitHub. It installs for the current user without administrator rights "
"(%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Programs\\Bookbench); as administrator \"for all users\" "
"can be chosen. Start menu entry, desktop shortcut optional. Uninstall in "
"the Windows settings under Apps; the settings in %APPDATA%\\Bookbench "
"stay.\n"
"- **Linux**: download the source code of the release (or clone the "
"repository) and run **./install.sh** in it. It copies the program to "
"~/.local/share/bookbench/programm, sets up a Python environment there "
"(venv) with the optional packages, a command **bookbench** in ~/.local/bin "
"and a menu entry. **./install.sh --uninstall** removes all that again; the "
"settings in ~/.config/bookbench stay. Needs Python 3.9 or newer and Qt for "
"Python (PySide6): if the system has it (Arch: sudo pacman -S pyside6), it "
"is used - then Bookbench looks like the other KDE programs and uses the KDE "
"file dialog; otherwise install.sh puts PySide6-Essentials into the Python "
"environment with pip (Debian/Ubuntu: python3-venv needed).\n"
"- Without installing: **python start.py** in the program folder; the "
"optional packages then come from pip (README).\n"
"\n"
"**Updating**\n"
"- **Help > Check for updates …** looks for the newest release on GitHub "
"([[einst_updates]]). Installed copies also look by themselves at start.\n"
"- A newer version shows its changes (formatted like on GitHub, links open "
"in the browser), with **Install now** for installed copies.\n"
"- **Install now** on Windows: downloads the setup, closes Bookbench, "
"installs the new version over the old one and starts it again.\n"
"- **Install now** on Linux: downloads the new version, replaces the program "
"folder and restarts Bookbench; the Python environment stays.\n"
"- From 1.x to 2.0 on Linux: the update only replaces the program files. If "
"PySide6 is missing afterwards, Bookbench says so at the next start and "
"offers to install it with pip (or: sudo pacman -S pyside6, then start "
"again).\n"
"- Before closing Bookbench asks about unsaved changes and ejecting, like "
"when quitting. Saying no keeps the download; Install now later uses it.\n"
"- The setup is checked against the file SHA256SUMS of the release; a "
"damaged download is not installed.\n"
"- **Skip this version**: no more window for it at start; the manual check "
"still shows it.\n"
"- A copy running from source code (git clone) is not replaced; update it "
"with git pull.\n"
"\n"
"The **desktop KOReader** for the preview (Linux only) is not part of the "
"installation ([[vorschau]]).\n"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1228
msgid "Troubleshooting"
msgstr ""

#: help_topics.py:1228
msgid ""
"\n"
"**KOReader still shows the old chapter names, blurb or cover**\n"
"- KOReader keeps its own copy of the book information. Cover browser > "
"long-press the book or menu > **Extract and cache book information** (or "
"for the whole folder). The table of contents is read anew when the book is "
"opened.\n"
"\n"
"**JPEG covers are not shown**\n"
"- The package **Pillow** is missing: pip install pillow. Without it only "
"PNG and GIF can be shown.\n"
"\n"
"**\"No API key\" or nothing goes to Claude**\n"
"- Key under [[einst_online]] or the environment variable ANTHROPIC_API_KEY; "
"package anthropic installed? Without a key only the free sources are "
"asked.\n"
"\n"
"**API error**\n"
"- The status bar shows Anthropic's message: no credit, wrong key, model "
"name unknown, overloaded. The book does not count as tried and is looked up "
"again next time.\n"
"\n"
"**Google Books / Open Library: network error**\n"
"- Behind a proxy or offline nothing can be reached; the book is not marked "
"as tried. Google sometimes limits requests without a key: try again later.\n"
"\n"
"**The e-reader is not found**\n"
"- On the e-reader confirm the connection (KOReader: USB mass storage, or "
"the device's own software: Connect).\n"
"- Newer Kindles and Android devices only connect via MTP, without a drive "
"([[kobo_geraete]]).\n"
"- Then wait a few seconds or E-reader menu > **Find e-reader**. Is the "
"drive mounted (Linux: file manager)? If needed, open the books folder on "
"the e-reader: then its root counts too.\n"
"\n"
"**Restored settings are gone again**\n"
"- KOReader writes its settings when it closes. Eject first, then start "
"KOReader - do not change anything in a KOReader that was already running.\n"
"\n"
"**A chapter level cannot be changed**\n"
"- nav.xhtml and toc.ncx of this book have a different number of entries; "
"the status bar names the reason ([[kapitel_ebenen]]).\n"
"\n"
"**Preview: window too large or patches fail**\n"
"- Choose \"Fitted to the screen\" ([[vorschau_bildschirm]]). Patches that "
"need Kobo hardware fail on the computer; the output under **Profile, device "
"and output** says which (workbench: vorschau/werkbank/koreader.log).\n"
"\n"
"**Preview: the workbench shows no picture**\n"
"- The line under ◀ ▶ says what KOReader reports. **Restart** tries again; a "
"profile from the e-reader is needed ([[vorschau_profil]]). If KOReader "
"itself does not start, the same causes as for the window apply (below).\n"
"\n"
"**Preview: KOReader closes at once (\"device/kobo/device.lua\" in the "
"output)**\n"
"- The desktop KOReader takes the computer for an e-reader: up to at least "
"version 2026.07 it recognizes the device by files such as /usr/bin/hwdetect "
"(Arch/EndeavourOS: package hwdetect), /bin/kobo_config.sh or /proc/usid. "
"KOReader alone does the same.\n"
"- Normally this does not happen in the preview: Bookbench puts a start "
"patch (1-bookbench-desktop.lua) into the profile that hides these files "
"from KOReader. It does not count as one of your patches and never goes to "
"the e-reader. It does not work if patches are switched off in KOReader; "
"then Bookbench says so and which file it is.\n"
"- Way out: KOReader as Flatpak (flatpak install flathub "
"rocks.koreader.KOReader), it does not see these files. Enter **flatpak** "
"under Settings > Device > Preview > Desktop KOReader ([[einst_vorschau]]), "
"so that it is taken even if KOReader is also installed normally. Or remove "
"the file if nothing else needs it.\n"
"\n"
"**Something else**\n"
"- The status bar always has the last message. Backups of all saved books "
